TI  - Sutra-PCM: Auditable Commonsense Reasoning with Architecturally Integrated
      Nyāya Explanations
SP  - 477-482
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Explanations added after training often drift from the evidence a model
      actually uses. We build Sutra-PCM, a small commonsense system that
      produces its rationale as it predicts. It pairs a frozen sentence encoder
      (all-mpnet-base-v2, 110M) with a 6-layer trainable module ( 2M). A
      Nyaya-style layer formats claim, evidence, support, and a light
      ConceptNet-derived scalar nudges option scores. On a mix of commonsense,
      ethical and logical tasks, Sutra-PCM reaches 53.6% accuracy versus 58.9%
      for BERT-base. This 5.3- point gap is expected: we trade a modest amount
      of accuracy for architecturally integrated auditability. We quantify
      faithfulness with ERASER on 500 validation examples (evaluated on model
      predictions, not gold labels). In this analysis, the selected spans keep
      most predicted probability mass, with a median sufficiency of 0.93. We
      also report calibrated uncertainty using temperature scaling and
      reliability diagrams. This design is not model-agnostic, but it yields
      consistent Nyaya- style rationales and clearer failure cases. In
      oversight-heavy decision support, such faithful, auditable traces can be
      more useful than a small gain in raw accuracy. We do not claim to
      guarantee ground truth; instead, we make the path to testing and auditing
      faithfulness explicit. © 2025 IEEE.
