TI  - The Role of Explainable AI for Healthcare 5.0: Best Practices, Challenges,
      and Opportunities
T2  - Edge AI for Industry 5.0 and Healthcare 5.0 Applications
SP  - 45-80
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - CRC Press
AB  - Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) is a way to improve transparency
      and understanding of artificial intelligence (AI) models. The use of AI in
      medicine is expanding rapidly. It has the potential to improve patient
      problems at a significantly lower cost. However, due to the complexity of
      AI models, it can be difficult to understand how they arrive at their
      conclusions, which can be a barrier to adoption of this overwhelming
      technology. XAI offers a solution to this problem by identifying potential
      flaws in AI models prior to certification, thereby improving the safety
      and effectiveness of AI-based medical systems. XAI’s main goal is to help
      people understand the reasons behind AI decisions and outcomes, promoting
      trust, credibility, and accountability. XAI is crucial in healthcare
      because it is critical that AI models used for clinical decision-making
      are transparent and provide human-explainable results. By detecting
      potential errors in AI models and providing suggestions for improvement,
      XAI can improve the adaptability and reliability of AI models. This
      ultimately leads to improved patient trust and an efficient healthcare
      system. To understand the basics of how XAI works, it can be categorized
      in different ways based on the desired results. These methods include
      rule-based, model-based, and post-explainability techniques. A rule-based
      method is a decision tree or system that makes decisions based on
      predefined rules. These rules can be written by subject matter experts or
      learned through machine learning algorithms. Model-based approaches use
      machine learning models such as decision trees and linear regression
      models designed for interpretation. Analyze model inputs and outputs using
      posterior explainability techniques to provide insight into how the model
      arrived at its decisions. These approaches justify the decisions made by
      the model by examining the results. XAI has a wide range of medical use
      cases, including medical image classification and segmentation, patient
      monitoring, and clinical decision support systems. Empowering physicians
      to make decisions by providing explanations for the results of AI-based
      models. XAI has the potential to improve medical outcomes by helping
      medical professionals detect, monitor, and treat complex diseases more
      quickly. The system improves clinical decision-making by justifying its
      recommendations, helping physicians make more informed and valuable
      decisions. For example, in the field of neurology, XAI is used to study
      EEG signals and identify changes in brain activity associated with
      epilepsy and other neurological disorders. In cardiology, XAI is used to
      analyze ECG signals and identify abnormalities that may indicate a
      potential risk of cardiovascular disease. In the field of oncology, XAI
      analyzes medical images to identify cancerous tumors and help predict
      disease progression. XAI can revolutionize the healthcare industry by
      improving disease detection, diagnosis, and treatment, ultimately
      improving patient outcomes. Nonetheless, adaptation of XAI may have some
      barriers that need to be overcome, such as the need for interpretable deep
      learning models and the ethical and legal implications associated with AI
      implementation. Addressing these challenges will enable XAI to be used
      responsibly and effectively for the benefit of patients and healthcare
      providers. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Pethuru Raj, B.
      Sundaravadivazhagan, A. Saleem Raja, and Mohammed M. Alani; individual
      chapters, the contributors.
