TI  - Artificial Intelligence in MOOC Analytics: A Critical Review of Prediction
      Methods and Future Directions
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Massive Open Online Courses (MOOCs) have become pivotal in democratizing
      education, offering flexible learning opportunities to diverse global
      audiences. With increasing learner data volumes, Artificial Intelligence
      (AI) is being actively integrated into MOOC platforms to support informed
      decision-making and personalized learning. This survey critically examines
      recent AI-driven approaches for predictive analytics in MOOC environments.
      Despite these advancements, MOOCs still grapple with issues such as high
      learner dropout rates, insufficient engagement modeling, limited
      personalization, and lack of transparency in automated decisions.
      Moreover, challenges in handling imbalanced data, preserving user privacy,
      and building generalizable models across heterogeneous learner populations
      persist. To address these challenges, we present an AI-powered MOOC
      analytics architecture that incorporates data collection, preprocessing,
      machine learning and deep learning models, and modules for dropout
      prediction, engagement forecasting, and adaptive recommendation. The
      architecture emphasizes ensemble and hybrid models, interpretable
      predictions, privacy-aware computation (e.g., federated learning), and
      actionable visual dashboards for educators. Evaluation strategies using
      metrics like ROC-AUC and F1-score are also reviewed to assess performance
      robustness. This survey highlights the research gaps and proposes future
      directions for developing ethical, explainable, and scalable AI systems
      tailored for online learning environments. © 2025 IEEE.
