TI  - The Double-Edged Sword: Navigating AI's Role in Banking Cybersecurity
SP  - 294-300
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) has fundamentally transformed cybersecurity
      in the banking sector, simultaneously providing advanced defensive
      capabilities and introducing new forms of risk. AI enables real-time
      detection of cyber-attacks, flags fraudulent financial activity by
      recognizing behavioral patterns, and automates threat responses. Key
      technologies like machine learning, natural language processing, and
      predictive analytics safeguard sensitive transactions and enhance
      resilience. However, these same advances empower cybercriminals, who
      exploit adversarial AI, deepfakes, and sophisticated phishing techniques
      to circumvent defenses and manipulate financial systems. Challenges such
      as data privacy, model explainability, algorithmic bias, and compliance
      with evolving regulations (e.g., GDPR, SOX, AI Act) further complicate AI
      adoption in banks. The growing ethical imperative mandates transparency,
      fairness, and accountability, especially in high stakes applications like
      credit scoring, anti-money laundering, and fraud detection. This article
      explores the dualistic role of AI in financial cybersecurity, the
      associated ethical and regulatory challenges, and advocates for a robust
      governance framework. Proposed measures include human-in-the-loop
      oversight, adversarial testing, continuous monitoring, and adherence to
      ethical standards. The future of AI in this domain hinges on striking a
      balance between leveraging innovation for protection and containing the
      evolving risks through proactive, multidisciplinary oversight and
      collaboration. © 2025 IEEE.
