TI  - A Two-Stage Framework for Explainable Clustering in Public-Sector
      Analytics: Algorithm Selection and Post-Hoc Validation
SP  - 2627-2634
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - We present and validate a two-stage framework for explainable clustering
      of public-sector units that balances technical validity and managerial
      adoption. Stage 1 formalizes algorithm selection as a transparent
      multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) problem that combines internal
      indices (Silhouette, Calinski-Harabasz, Davies-Bouldin) with stability
      (Adjusted Rand Index), scalability (median runtime), and interpretability.
      Stage 2 withholds a business outcome and conducts post-hoc validation via
      ANOVA/Welch and Tukey/Games-Howell. On 59,444 unit-month records from a
      large public financial institution, the framework selects K-means (k=7)
      and identifies seven organizational archetypes with statistically
      significant differences in financial performance. We report 20 independent
      runs per configuration, fixed seeds, and reproducible settings. The
      archetypes support targeted HR interventions and evidence-based
      management, empirically reinforcing Contingency Theory (fit between
      workforce and territorial context). The study illustrates how explainable
      clustering can translate large-scale administrative data into trustworthy,
      auditable decision support for government applications. © 2025 IEEE.
