TI  - Designing a Human-in-the-Loop Framework for AI-Augmented Control Room
      Management System in Grid Operation
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The increasing complexity of power grid operations calls for advanced
      tools that support real-time decision-making, operational traceability,
      and human-machine collaboration. While Artificial Intelligence (AI) offers
      scalable decision support, control room environments demand a careful
      balance between automation and human judgment. This paper proposes a
      Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) AI integration and evaluation framework before
      adopting AI in critical sectors such as grid operation. A case study on
      HITL-AI evaluation the development and deployment of a web-based Control
      Room Management System (CRMS), designed to streamline shift log keeping,
      event documentation, manpower allocation, and element outage coordination.
      By digitizing these critical workflows, the system enhances situational
      awareness, accountability, and efficiency among grid operators.
      Furthermore, the paper outlines a Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) framework for
      integrating Artificial Intelligence (AI) capabilities into CRMS - such as
      predictive analytics, natural language event logging, and AI-assisted
      report generation - while preserving human judgment at the core of
      operational control. The paper also evaluates current AI technologies
      relevant to grid operations and highlights framework for evaluation of
      potential risks associated with over-reliance on autonomous systems. This
      proposed framework advocates responsible A I adoption that complements
      rather than replaces human expertise in critical power system
      environments. © 2025 IEEE.
