TI  - Federated and Explainable Intrusion Detection Systems for Large-Scale
      Heterogeneous Networks using Ensemble Deep Learning
SP  - 1133-1139
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The swift advancement of cyberattacks, particularly zero-day
      vulnerabilities, presents considerable difficulties for intrusion
      detection systems (IDSs) within 5G/6G and IoT-enabled networks. Current
      deep learning-based Intrusion Detection Systems, while successful,
      frequently exhibit deficiencies in scalability, interpretability, and
      robustness within heterogeneous and resource-limited contexts. This
      research presents a federated ensemble Intrusion Detection System (IDS)
      that amalgamates Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Long Short-Term
      Memory (LSTM) networks, and Transformers to concurrently record
      geographical, temporal, and contextual patterns in network traffic.
      Federated learning with aggregation algorithms is implemented to
      facilitate privacy-preserving training. Model transparency is improved
      using explainable AI (XAI) tools, such as SHAP, LIME, and counterfactual
      explanations, which offer interpretable decision assistance. Additionally,
      a GAN-based adversarial defense is integrated to enhance robustness
      against evasion attacks. The model undergoes thorough evaluation on
      benchmark datasets (NSL-KDD, UNSW-NB15, CICIDS2017, CSE-CIC-IDS2018,
      TON-IoT20, Edge-IIoTset) and is validated on a practical IoT edge testbed
      utilizing Raspberry Pi and Jetson Nano devices. Experimental findings
      indicate exceptional predictive performance (accuracy reaching 98.5%,
      AUC-ROC exceeding 0.99), enhanced adversarial resilience, and efficient
      inference on edge devices. The suggested methodology provides a scalable,
      interpretable, and robust IDS framework appropriate for advanced
      intelligent networks. © 2025 IEEE.
