TI  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Emergency Medicine: Balancing
      Opportunities and Challenges
T2  - JMIR Med. Inform.
JF  - JMIR Medical Informatics
VL  - 13
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - JMIR Publications Inc.
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs)
      such as ChatGPT, has rapidly evolved and is reshaping various fields,
      including clinical medicine. Emergency medicine stands to benefit from
      AI’s capacity for high-volume data processing, workflow optimization, and
      clinical decision support. However, important challenges exist, ranging
      from model “hallucinations” and data bias to questions of
      interpretability, liability, and ethical use in high-stake environments.
      This updated viewpoint provides a structured overview of AI’s current
      capabilities in emergency medicine, highlights real-world applications,
      and explores concerns regarding regulatory requirements, safety standards,
      and transparency (explainable AI). We discuss the potential risks and
      limitations of LLMs, including their performance in rare or atypical
      presentations common in the emergency department and potential biases that
      could disproportionately affect vulnerable populations. We also address
      the regulatory landscape, particularly the liability for AI-driven
      decisions, and emphasize the need for clear guidelines and human
      oversight. Ultimately, AI holds enormous promise for improving patient
      care and resource management in emergency medicine; however, ensuring
      safety, fairness, and accountability remains vital. © Félix Amiot, Benoit
      Potier.
