TI  - Hacking the Black Box: Prompt Injection for Visualizing LLM Reasoning as
      Graphs
SP  - 303-306
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) show impressive versatility in translation,
      summarization, dialogue generation, and reasoning; however, their internal
      inference mechanisms remain opaque. This lack of transparency limits trust
      and poses challenges in education, healthcare, and decision support, where
      interpretability and accountability are essential features. To address
      this, we propose a lightweight framework that uses prompt injection to
      induce LLMs to explicitly output their reasoning traces. These traces,
      which contain assumptions, intermediate steps, and dependencies, are
      automatically parsed and visualized as directed graphs, enabling users to
      inspect the reasoning process step-by-step. Unlike traditional XAI
      methods, such as LIME or SHAP, which focus on feature attribution, our
      approach captures the sequential reasoning flow characteristic of
      generative models. By generating multiple traces for the same input and
      comparing their structural similarities, the framework also provides a
      quantitative measure of reasoning stability and reproducibility.
      Experiments with the Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite model showed that the method
      effectively externalizes reasoning paths and maintains task focus, even
      when prompts include distracting content. Although self-reported traces
      may not fully reflect internal computations, the proposed framework offers
      a practical and interpretable window into model behavior. It supports
      visual auditing, transparency analysis, and educational applications,
      contributing to a deeper alignment between human reasoning and machine
      cognition. ©2025 IEEE.
