TI  - Illuminating Predictions: The Use and Evaluation of Explainable AI in
      Predictive Process Monitoring - A Systematic Review
VL  - 270
SP  - 1149-1158
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Elsevier B.V.
AB  - Predictive Process Monitoring (PPM) enables organizations to anticipate
      and manage future business events using machine learning models. However,
      the widespread adoption of PPM is hindered by the lack of transparency in
      these models, often referred to as the "black-box problem." Explainable
      Artificial Intelligence (XAI) addresses this challenge by providing
      interpretability techniques that enhance trust, accountability, and
      regulatory compliance. This systematic review explores the
      state-of-the-art XAI methods applied in PPM, focusing on their integration
      with AI models, the types of PPM tasks they support, and the evaluation
      approaches used to assess explainability. The findings reveal a strong
      preference for post-hoc explainability methods, particularly SHAP-based
      feature importance explanations, with a growing interest in graph-based
      and visualization techniques. Additionally, while evaluating AI models is
      standard practice, systematic evaluation of explainers remains
      underdeveloped, with no dominant metric emerging. This review highlights
      key research gaps and suggests future directions for improving
      explainability evaluation and integrating intrinsic explainability into
      PPM systems. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.. All rights reserved.
