TI  - Explainable Xgboost for Clinician-Acceptable Diabetes Risk Prediction
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - However, reliable decision support tools for clinical practice should
      offer both precision as well as an explanatory rationale behind that
      prediction. Here, we present Explainable XGBoost for Clinician-Acceptable
      Diabetes Risk Prediction, which is a completely transparent pipeline that
      employs machine learning for predicting the risk of diabetes based on
      routine clinical health indicators along with the rationale for every
      prediction visible by explainable AI (XAI) methodologies. An XGBoost
      classifier is trained using data from the BRFSS2015 dataset, which
      contains 253,680 survey responses according to 21 health indicators, with
      the target variable Diabetes-0-2 categorizing individuals according to
      three classes: no diabetes or gestational diabetes (0), prediabetes (1),
      and diabetes (2). The categorical features are one-hot encoded, and the
      diabetes classes are label encoded, enabling the model to capture complex
      non-linear relationships without introducing any artificial ordering to
      the data. Stratified splits (70 % train, 15 % validation, and 15 % test)
      were carried out to ensure balanced evaluation of the model's performance.
      The model achieves an accuracy of over 85% and log-loss <0.4 on the
      held-out test set, demonstrating clinical significance. In order to fill
      the 'black-box' gap, we incorporate two complementary XAI methods:. SHAP
      provides a global view, ranking health indicators according to their
      average contribution to each diabetes-related class. From here, clinicians
      could quickly appreciate that BMI, physical activity, and age are the main
      predictors of patients' classifications, in agreement with medical wisdom.
      The two additional layered explanations empower physicians not only to
      weigh the model's overall performance but also to evaluate every specific
      patient's decision. The predictive power of XGBoost thus strikes a good
      balance between transparency, accountability, and trust with the
      explainability of SHAP and LIME; it is a promising step toward winning
      clinician acceptance of AI and helping realize safer and more effective
      diabetes risk prediction in clinical settings. © 2025 IEEE.
