TI  - Farmer-Centric Artificial Intelligence through Explainable Federated
      Learning for Smart Agriculture
T2  - Agricultural Supply Chain Optimization Using Federated Learning
SP  - 299-322
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Wiley
AB  - Agriculture is changing more than ever before because of new technologies
      such as artificial intelligence (AI), the Internet of Things, and
      data-driven decision-making. However, farmers often see AI solutions as a
      complex “black box” system, which poses many challenges involving trust,
      adoption, and effective deployment in agricultural practice. In this
      paper, we propose a framework based on a farmer-centric principle to
      integrate explainable federated learning (XFL) in agricultural systems,
      thereby providing transparency and accountability in AI-based decision
      support, as well as trustworthiness. This federated approach also aims to
      preserve farmer data privacy, while the intelligent collaborative system
      enables interactive learning from decentralized training inputs from
      numerous data sources. This framework incorporates the concept of XFL and
      addresses important elements in smart farming, such as yield prediction,
      intelligent irrigation inputs, soil health observations, and agricultural
      supply chain management. This paper also investigates state-of-the-art
      explainable AI methods, including feature attribution, interpretable deep
      learning, and model-agnostic explanation processes in agriculture.
      Moreover, we propose a multilayer architecture whereby the federated
      models offer value in prediction while the explainability modules provide
      the outputs in terms that are more comprehensible and useful to farmers
      and their operations. The socio-technical dimension is highlighted here to
      ensure that AI systems provide inputs that fit in with farmer
      requirements, regulatory measures, and sustainable development policies.
      The use of XFL enhances the elements of trust, adoption, and resilience in
      agricultural systems, which have been highlighted in numerous case studies
      and simulated scenarios. © 2026 Scrivener Publishing LLC.
