TI  - Avoiding the Knowledge-Graph-on-Hot-Path Anti-Pattern: A
      Separation-of-Truth Architecture for Real-Time COP in Battlefield
      Simulation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Real-time decision support in system-of-systems operations depends on a
      continuously updated Common Operational Picture (COP) that remains
      responsive, deterministic, and auditable under stochastic and
      heterogeneous inputs. In Battlefield Management Simulation Systems (BMSS),
      we structure the pipeline as a five-layer semantic architecture (input
      ingestion, knowledge, simulation, AI analysis, and
      visualization/human-in-the-loop), where an ontology-backed knowledge graph
      (KG) provides a shared semantic contract for scenario authoring,
      capability constraints, and provenance-aware explanation. However, placing
      KG persistence or reasoning on the COP hot path amplifies tail latency and
      destabilizes tick execution. We propose Separation-of-Truth (SoT-split),
      which decouples real-time COP state serving from semantic truth: the
      simulation loop reads/writes a low-latency COP state store, appends
      immutable events to an event log, and materializes KG views asynchronously
      for postrun audit and analytics. We validate logical outcome stability via
      a 500-run Monte Carlo campaign on the Forest Assault scenario, where Blue
      achieves 31.2% wins (156 / 500) with a 95% Wilson confidence interval (CI)
      of [27.3%, 35.4%]. To quantify the KG-on-hot-path anti-pattern, we run a
      controlled microbenchmark that emulates inline semantic persistence and
      scan operations; at 4 × workload scale, SoT-split reduces perturn p95
      latency from 0.564 ms to 0.110 ms(5.1 ×) and p99 from 0.700 ms to 0.149
      ms(4.7 ×), while increasing throughput from 2,175 to 10,724 turns/s. These
      results indicate that SoT-split preserves real-time COP responsiveness
      while retaining semantic rigor and replayable auditability. © 2026 IEEE.
