TI  - Trust Aware and Explainable Access Control for Internet of Medical Things:
      A Lightweight Hybrid Blockchain Approach
T2  - IEEE Internet Things J.
JF  - IEEE Internet of Things Journal
VL  - 13
IS  - 12
SP  - 27843-27861
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) permits continuous patient
      monitoring, remote diagnosis, and intelligent clinical decision support
      using networked medical devices. However, IoMT environments are very
      challenging, given their stringent latency requirements, device diversity,
      and dynamic clinical workflows, which hinder traditional static access
      control (AC) approaches from performing effectively. Traditional AC
      systems, such as role-based AC (RBAC) and fixed attribute-based AC (ABAC),
      fail to adapt to real-time contextual changes, evolving user behavior, and
      trust dynamics in medical Internet of Things (IoT) systems. In addition,
      AI-driven AC solutions often operate as closed box models, limiting
      transparency and regulatory compliance. This article proposes a trust
      aware and explainable AC framework specifically designed for IoMT systems,
      integrating a tree-based ensemble learning model for trust evaluation,
      explainable artificial intelligence (XAI), and a lightweight hybrid
      blockchain architecture suitable for real-time IoMT deployments. The
      framework dynamically evaluates access requests using contextual and
      behavioral attributes and generates human-interpretable explanations for
      each access decision using SHAP-based XAI, enabling transparency and
      compliance with healthcare regulations. To meet IoMT latency and
      scalability requirements, computationally intensive machine learning (ML)
      and explainability tasks are executed off-chain, while access decisions
      and audit logs are immutably recorded using a hybrid blockchain composed
      of Hyperledger Fabric and Hyperledger Besu. A prototype implementation in
      a realistic IoMT environment demonstrates that the proposed framework
      achieves an access decision accuracy of 93.4%, with an average end-to-end
      latency of 1.8 s, which is suitable for real-time medical workflows. The
      off-chain AI integration reduces blockchain execution costs by 81%
      compared to fully on-chain solutions, while preserving security,
      auditability, and system reliability. These results indicate that trust
      aware and explainable AC can significantly enhance the security,
      transparency, and operational robustness of next-generation IoMT systems.
      © 2026 IEEE.
