TI  - Explainable Multimodal Systems in Electronic Health Records and Predictive
      Analytics
T2  - Enhancing Healthcare Informatics with Transparent and Explainable AI
SP  - 62-87
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - CRC Press
AB  - The increasing reliance on artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare has
      necessitated the expansion of explainable AI (XAI) techniques,
      particularly in diagnostics, treatment recommendations, and personalized
      medicine. XAI plays a pivotal role in addressing the opacity of complex AI
      models by offering transparency and explainability to their
      decision-making processes. This is especially crucial in critical
      applications of multimodal health predictions such as drug discovery,
      electronic health record (EHR) analytics, and personalized treatment
      plans, where trust, accountability, and ethical compliance are paramount.
      Multimodal explainable AI (MXAI) techniques are capable of handling
      multiple modalities or types of healthcare data such as textual data from
      EHRs and lab reports; medical images such as X-rays, MRIs, CT scans,
      ultrasound images; audio data such as heartbeat sound; sensor data
      obtained from wearable devices and genomic data obtained from genetic
      sequencing and other molecular biology data that help in
      pharmacovigilance. Despite the diverse potential of MXAI, there are
      significant challenges that are hindering the widespread adoption of MXAI
      in healthcare. One major obstacle is the scalability of MXAI methods for
      large and heterogeneous datasets like EHRs, which often contain irregular,
      incomplete, or inconsistent data across different modalities. Another
      pressing issue is the trade-off between the high predictive performance of
      complex AI models and their reasonability, as many high-performing systems
      function as “black boxes”. Ethical and privacy concerns further complicate
      the deployment of MXAI, particularly when the explanations involve
      sensitive patient data or reveal biases in clinical decisions. To overcome
      these challenges, the chapter discusses an integrated novel framework
      based on recent advancements in MXAI techniques and multimodal EHR
      modeling. This framework utilizes multimodal AI systems for analyzing (i)
      structured data such as laboratory results and (ii) unstructured data such
      as EHRs, medical history, and clinical notes, through advanced temporal
      modeling techniques and interleaved attention mechanisms. These techniques
      address data irregularities and provide interpretable predictions.
      Incorporating causal inference techniques within MXAI frameworks further
      elucidates relationships between medical concepts from EHRs and drug
      recommendations, while multimodal fusion with attention-based methods
      enables the system to account for interactions across data modalities.
      This framework directly addresses the current limitations of MXAI. By
      combining scalable modeling techniques with comprehensive explanations,
      the framework ensures adaptability to large-scale and diverse healthcare
      datasets. Advanced temporal and causal models effectively handle
      irregularities in time series and textual data while safeguarding
      sensitive patient information through privacy-preserving mechanisms. This
      framework could aid in explainable drug discovery and pharmacovigilance.
      Moreover, this chapter also comprehensively portrays recent advancements
      in the field of MXAI in healthcare encompassing methods involving multiple
      data modalities for both primary prediction and explanation tasks. The
      chapter specifically highlights AI-driven prediction tasks and publicly
      available datasets used to develop and evaluate explanations in multimodal
      contexts. Additionally, the chapter offers a thorough exploration of the
      current state of the art in MXAI for drug discovery. It delves into
      diverse XAI methodologies, their applications in EHR analysis, and the
      challenges and limitations associated with implementing these techniques
      in the domain. This integration of MXAI into clinical workflows bridges
      the gap between predictive performance and interpretability, paving the
      way for AI systems to become indispensable tools in healthcare.
      Ultimately, this strategy enhances patient outcomes, strengthens clinician
      confidence, and accelerates the ethical adoption of AI in medical
      practice. © 2027 selection and editorial matter, Philip Eappen, Narasimha
      Rao Vajjhala, Ruiling Guo, Lucy Shinners, and Virginia Gunn; individual
      chapters, the contributors.
