AB  - Personalised food safety applies consumer- and context-specific data to
      move beyond one-size-fits-all controls towards tailored, timely guidance
      that reduces risk and supports healthier eating. This chapter surveys the
      technologies and practices that enable this shift. We outline the data
      foundations, health and biological measures, purchasing histories, and
      social/behavioural/environmental signals, as well as emerging sources from
      wearables, smart kitchens, and connected retail. We review AI-driven
      capabilities, including explainable recommendation systems, vision- and
      sensor-assisted meal logging, NLP/LLM chatbots that parse labels and menus
      against individual constraints, and predictive analytics that forecast
      cross-contact or operational hazards. IoT devices personalise monitoring
      and alerts in homes and food service, while adaptive menus and
      mass-customised products align safety with preference, culture, and
      budget. We also examine governance, including privacy-by-design, consent
      and user control, secure data integration, and compliance with the
      GDPR/CCPA, and the EU AI Act. Case examples illustrate gains in recall
      speed, diet adherence, and reduced waste, alongside persistent
      challenges—data quality, interoperability, SME adoption costs, and equity.
      We conclude with a roadmap for multidisciplinary collaboration to scale
      safe, ethical personalisation across the food system. © 2027 John Wiley &
      Sons Ltd.
