AB  - Biomedical imaging has been playing a pivotal role in modern diagnosis,
      treatment selections, and clinical monitoring, but conventional
      interpretation is time-consuming and subjective. Advancement in artificial
      intelligence (AI)-based developments are revolutionizing the entire
      imaging pipeline, including acquisition and reconstruction, quantitative
      analysis, and predictive modeling. The emerging deep learning
      architectures, such as the convolutional neural network, the transformer,
      and the generative model, are improving the quality of the images, making
      it possible to perform a low dose of the images, automatically reduce and
      diagnose cancer, and discover clinically relevant biomarkers. The
      integration of imaging with electronic health records and genomic data and
      continuously measured physiological signals into the multimodal approach
      can further contribute to a transition to personalized medicine that is
      more predictive. Advanced technologies like foundational models, federated
      learning, and explainable AI extend scalability, collaboration, and
      clinical interpretability. Despite its promises, AI in imaging brings
      challenges around data privacy, regulatory endorsement, algorithmic
      fairness, and smooth integration into clinical workflows. This chapter
      identifies the current trends, case studies, and further directions to
      show how AI can move biomedical imaging beyond descriptive visualization
      toward biologically sensitive, patient-specific decision-making. Overall,
      AI represents not just a technological supple ment, but a paradigm shifts
      toward more accurate, fair, and opinionated healthcare. © 2026 Walter de
      Gruyter GmbH, Genthiner Straße 13, 10785 Berlin, Germany.
