TI  - EVCA-Net: An MDN-Based Explainable Framework for Robust Secure Profiling
      in Smart EV Charging Infrastructure
T2  - Cybern. Syst.
JF  - Cybernetics and Systems
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Taylor and Francis Ltd.
AB  - The rapid proliferation of Electric Vehicle (EV) charging infrastructure
      within smart grid ecosystems has exposed these cyber-physical assets to
      complex threats that compromise data privacy, billing integrity, and
      hardware safety. Conventional intrusion detection mechanisms struggle with
      non-linear attack vectors, lack structural interpretability, and impose
      high computational overheads. To overcome these challenges, this paper
      presents EVCA-Net, a novel, high-performance hybrid expert system
      framework for automated EV charger threat profiling and explainable
      security diagnostics. Validated on physical Raspberry Pi-managed control
      unit telemetry using the CIC-EVSE-2024 dataset, EVCA-Net deploys an
      advanced preprocessing pipeline and a Transformer-driven Mixture Density
      Network (T-MDN) module to extract deep multimodal spatiotemporal features.
      To optimize computational efficiency, a hybrid feature-selection strategy
      combines Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) with Marine Predator
      Optimization (MPO) to isolate critical threat indicators. Classification
      is executed using a Deep Additive Neural Network (DANet) integrated with
      Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME), delivering
      transparent, operator-verifiable security maps. EVCA-Net achieves 99.95%
      accuracy along with 99.639% precision, 99.266% recall, and 99.452%
      F1-score, establishing a robust, scalable, and fully explainable
      decision-support paradigm for modern EV charging infrastructure. © 2026
      Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
