TI  - Large language model-driven semantic clustering and zero-shot segmentation
      with structured query language-embedded learning from examples module 2
      rules for analysing tax reform acceptance
T2  - Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell.
JF  - Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 182
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Decision support systems based on large language models (LLMs) are
      evolving rapidly, and one important design direction is to complement them
      with stable and interpretable machine learning components, such as
      rule-based systems. In this work, we present a decision support system
      framework that combines semantic clustering based on LLM embeddings and
      zero-shot semantic segmentation with explainable decision rules induced by
      the Learn From Examples by Modules 2 (LEM2) algorithm and embedded in an
      SQL database. An additional contribution of this work is a dedicated
      mechanism for embedding LEM2 rules in a Structured Query Language (SQL)
      environment, combined with a query formulation that enables classification
      to be performed as a single SQL query per object. The proposed framework
      enables the transformation of unstructured textual survey data into a
      discrete and understandable set of rules that can be directly used in
      database-driven decision support systems. The framework has potential
      applications in LLM-assisted user interaction systems and can be adapted
      to other survey-based decision problems, including citizen feedback
      systems and similar public-sector decision-support scenarios. The case
      study is based on unpublished survey data concerning the level of
      acceptance of proposed changes to property tax. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
