TI  - Towards an Adaptive and Intelligent Dashboard for AI-Driven Clinical
      Decision Support
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Clinical AI dashboards can support risk prediction, patient
      prioritization, and clinicians' decision-making. However, moving such
      systems from prototype to clinical use requires more than accurate
      predictions. The dashboards should support adaptive configuration,
      guideline governance, confidence-aware recommendation delivery, privacy
      control, and human-in-the-loop feedback. This paper presents an adaptive
      architecture for a hybrid machine learning and large language model
      clinical AI dashboard for hypoxemia and hypertension risk prediction.
      Using a design-science approach, we translated clinicians' concerns and
      deployment requirements into adaptive intelligent-system features and
      operationalized them through a three-layer architecture: a predictive
      intelligence layer, an explanation and recommendation layer, and an
      adaptive control layer. The adaptive control layer governs how AI
      recommendations are configured, displayed, justified, filtered, and
      refined through clinician interaction. We instantiate the architecture
      through a dashboard prototype and a feedback-to-requirement mapping that
      shows how clinicians' concerns were translated into system mechanisms for
      guideline-source configuration, explanation personalization,
      confidence-threshold adjustment, privacy and security control, and
      structured accept/modify/override feedback. This work contributes a
      clinician-informed adaptive architecture for AI clinical decision support
      and demonstrates how dashboard-level intelligence can support localized
      guideline alignment, transparent recommendation delivery, clinician
      oversight, and continuous system refinement for safer clinical AI
      adoption. © 2026 IEEE.
