TI  - Adaptive and secure multimodal federated learning for distributed
      cardiovascular risk stratification with interpretable clinical decision
      support
T2  - Cluster Comput.
JF  - Cluster Computing
VL  - 29
IS  - 11
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Springer
AB  - The use of machine learning models for cardiovascular risk assessment in
      decentralized healthcare settings is hindered by data silos, privacy
      constraints, multimodal heterogeneity, and restricted interpretability.
      Federated learning facilitates collaborative model training without the
      centralization of raw data; nevertheless, current methodologies frequently
      depend on static aggregation techniques, inadequate preprocessing
      frameworks, and insufficient empirical validation across diverse
      modalities. This paper presents an adaptable and secure multimodal
      federated learning system intended for distributed cardiovascular risk
      prediction inside clustered healthcare infrastructures. The proposed
      architecture includes: (i) modality-aware feature extraction for
      structured electronic health records, temporal wearable sensor signals,
      and cardiac imaging representations; (ii) a heterogeneity-sensitive
      preprocessing pipeline comprising statistical normalization,
      denoising-based feature refinement, and class-imbalance mitigation via
      synthetic minority augmentation; and (iii) an adaptive aggregation
      mechanism that dynamically re-weights client updates according to data
      distribution divergence and local validation stability. The methodology
      improves clinical transparency by incorporating post-hoc interpretability
      using Shapley-based feature attribution, facilitating both global and
      individualized risk explanations. Comprehensive experimental assessments
      are performed on both real-world and benchmark cardiovascular datasets.
      Comparative analyses encompass centralized learning, traditional FedAvg,
      various convolutional architectures, and alternative optimization methods
      to evaluate convergence behavior and computing efficiency. Ablation
      research measures the impact of each architectural element. The results
      indicate persistent enhancements in AUC, F1-score, Matthews Correlation
      Coefficient, balanced accuracy, and convergence stability in the presence
      of class imbalance. An analysis of computational overhead and
      communication costs is conducted to evaluate the viability of
      implementation in clustered medical systems. Privacy considerations are
      analyzed through the exploration of potential federated vulnerabilities
      and corresponding mitigation techniques. The results indicate that
      adaptive multimodal federated modeling, along with systematic
      preprocessing and interpretable decision support, offers a scalable and
      therapeutically feasible approach for distributed cardiovascular risk
      stratification. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer
      Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
