AB  - Natural language processing (NLP), being one of the fundamental fields of
      artificial intelligence, has become a revolutionary technology in
      healthcare by empowering machines to process and comprehend human language
      in the healthcare setting. The explosive increase in unstructured medical
      data—clinical notes, radiology reports, discharge summaries, and patient
      feedback—has made it inevitable to use intelligent tools to analyze and
      extract in an efficient manner. NLP technologies have shown their promise
      in transforming this enormous reservoir of text data into structured forms
      that support clinical decision-making, improve patient care, and
      streamline healthcare operations. The present review discusses the
      development of NLP in healthcare, with some of the major trends being the
      use of deep learning models, interoperability with electronic health
      records (EHRs), and increasing use of transformer-based models like BERT
      and BioBERT. It spans uses of NLP for information extraction,
      computer-assisted coding, clinical documentation improvement, predictive
      analytics, and even mental illness screening via sentiment analysis and
      emotional evaluation. Real-world applications, such as virtual health
      assistants, chatbots, and decision-support systems, underscore the growing
      use of NLP to streamline clinical workflows and enhance patient
      engagement. Though promising, the medical application of NLP is faced with
      various primary issues including data privacy, language complexities of
      domains, and the availability of high-quality annotated data. Moreover,
      there are also challenges with model interpretability and transfer to
      different clinical contexts. This chapter continues to outline regulatory
      and ethical issues crucial in providing secure and responsible use of NLP
      within medicare setups. In the future, the horizon for NLP in healthcare
      lies in more robust multilingual models, real-time processing, and open
      integration with other AI technologies such as computer vision and
      robotics. Advances in explainable AI (XAI) and federated learning could
      further ease current limitations, paving the way to more secure and
      scalable NLP solutions. This review aims to provide a comprehensive
      overview of current possibilities, challenges, and opportunities that will
      constitute the next generation of NLP-based healthcare systems. © 2027
      selection and editorial matter, Abhinav Juneja, Manish Bhardwaj and Samad
      Noeiaghdam; individual chapters, the contributors.
