TI  - Between algorithm and agora: Ethical challenges in AI-assisted city
      planning – A systematic review and conceptual framework
T2  - Prog. Plann.
JF  - Progress in Planning
VL  - 203
IS  - 2
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly embedded in urban planning
      through predictive analytics, digital platforms, and algorithmic
      decision-support. While these tools can enhance analytical capacity, they
      also introduce ethical risks that are often only addressed implicitly by
      existing frameworks. This study conducts a systematic literature review of
      85 studies on AI-assisted planning scholarship to synthesize five
      interrelated ethical challenges: algorithmic redlining, digital exclusion,
      data sovereignty, algorithmic opacity (addressed through AI governance and
      algorithmic transparency), and professional ethical responsibility. The
      synthesis reveals that algorithmic redlining and digital exclusion are the
      most extensively theorised challenges in the existing literature, whereas
      data sovereignty and professional ethical responsibility remain
      comparatively underdeveloped; indicating a significant gap between the
      governance demands of AI-assisted planning and the profession-specific
      frameworks available to address them. The synthesis shows that these risks
      are mutually reinforcing, as participation inequalities and biased
      training data interact to produce unfair outcomes, while opaque vendor
      systems can weaken accountability and public trust. Building on these
      findings, the paper proposes a profession-facing ethical framework
      grounded in the shared professional conduct principles of planning bodies
      across Australia, the United Kingdom, the United States, and Canada. It
      argues that planners must act as ethically informed stewards of
      algorithmic governance by strengthening algorithmic literacy, documenting
      data provenance, demanding explainability, and maintaining inclusive
      engagement pathways. The framework is designed to be transferable across
      comparable Western planning systems. The findings have important
      implications for urban planners and planning professionals, as well as for
      researchers, by highlighting the need to strengthen ethical competencies,
      develop context-specific governance approaches, and further advance
      interdisciplinary research on AI-assisted urban planning. © 2026 The
      Authors
