TI  - SmartForest: Optimizing anemia diagnosis with enhanced random forest and
      XGBoost integration
T2  - Results Eng.
JF  - Results in Engineering
VL  - 27
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Elsevier B.V.
AB  - This study addresses the challenges in healthcare classification, focusing
      specifically on the diagnosis of anemia. It introduces SmartForest, a
      novel ensemble model that integrates Random Forest, XGBoost, and sea horse
      optimization techniques to enhance both accuracy and interpretability. By
      incorporating explainable artificial intelligence (AI) through SHAP
      analysis, the model aims to support transparency and trust in clinical
      decision-making, thereby contributing to improved patient outcomes.
      SmartForest leverages a soft voting mechanism to aggregate the probability
      estimates from both ensemble learners, ensuring balanced and interpretable
      predictions. The sea horse optimization algorithm is employed to determine
      the optimal number and depth of trees, facilitating robust performance
      across diverse medical datasets. Experimental results demonstrate that
      SmartForest outperforms traditional models across multiple evaluation
      metrics. On the anemia dataset, it achieves an accuracy of 0.9882,
      precision of 0.9809, recall of 0.9939, and F1-score of 0.9872. The model
      also exhibits strong discriminatory power with a ROC-AUC of 0.9982, and
      produces minimal prediction error, reflected by a low MSE of 0.0118 and
      RMSE of 0.0799. While the average prediction time is slightly higher at
      0.2501 seconds, this is a reasonable trade-off given the model's superior
      performance. The objective of this work was to improve anemia diagnosis
      using machine learning models, with SmartForest demonstrating the most
      promising results. Future research directions include expanding the
      dataset to include more diverse patient populations, integrating the model
      into real-world clinical decision support systems, enhancing model
      explainability, and exploring its applicability to other medical
      conditions. Ethical considerations surrounding data privacy and clinical
      deployment should also be addressed in future studies. © 2025 The
      Author(s)
