TI  - Inclusive Multimodal Fraud Detection for Differently Abled Users in
      Digital Banking
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - AI-driven fraud detection systems in digital banking commonly assume
      uniform user behavior, often overlooking accessibility-driven interaction
      patterns, thereby disadvantaging differently abled users, senior citizens,
      and other vulnerable populations. This study proposes an Inclusive
      Multimodal Fraud Detection (IMFD) framework that integrates
      accessibility-aware signals, including voice biometrics, screen reader
      navigation behavior, haptic interaction patterns, and eye-tracking data,
      into a hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture with attention-based multimodal
      fusion. The framework was evaluated using a mixed-methods approach that
      combined simulated banking environments, public fraud datasets, and
      controlled user trials involving differently abled participants.
      Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves an
      overall accuracy of 96% under cross-validated settings, while
      significantly reducing false-positive fraud alerts for vulnerable user
      groups and maintaining a low-latency real-time performance. Embedded
      explainability, implemented through attention mechanisms and SHAP-based
      feature attribution, provides transparent and interpretable fraud
      decisions, supporting regulatory alignment with accessibility and
      data-protection standards. By explicitly modeling assistive interaction
      behaviors rather than treating them as anomalies, this study demonstrates
      how inclusive and explainable AI can enable secure, equitable, and
      scalable fraud detection in digital banking systems. © 2026 IEEE.
