TI  - Smart Data Governance: A Hybrid Deep-Machine Learning Model for Real-Time
      Privacy and Compliance Automation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The emergence of IoT, IIoMT and smart-city infrastructures has resulted in
      data streams which are enormous, heterogeneous and need real-time control
      yet maintain privacy, security and regulatory compliance. Traditional
      centralized analytics solutions have high predictive performance, but are
      finding it harder and harder to remain relevant over time as
      data-sovereignty limits, high-privacy concerns, and lack of transparent
      auditability emerge. To overcome these shortcomings, this paper presents
      BC-FedHVAE-GRU, a blockchain-based federated hybrid deeplearning
      architecture that can be used to enable privacyconscious, compliant
      smart-data governance automation. The suggested system combines
      Hierarchical Variational Autoencoder (HVAE) to abstract privacy-sensitive
      features, a sequence modelling module that is based on GRU and that allows
      the representation of dynamic behavior and characterizing anomalies, and a
      federated learning (FL) architecture that allows learning without
      transferring raw data across different nodes. It includes a lightweight
      blockchain layer, which stores model changes immutably and enforces access
      permissions and offers audit traces that are complianceready based on
      smart contracts. Extensive experiments with IoT-like sensor datasets prove
      that BC-FedHVAE-GRU provides competitive methods of detection and has
      important privacy and governance benefits. The F1-scores of the
      centralized baselines were 0.942,0.859 and 0.707 with MLP, GRU, and
      CNN+GRU, respectively. The proposed model achieved 0.889 accuracy and
      0.864 F1-score under the federated conditions exceeding all the sequence
      based baselines and close to centralized performance regardless of
      decentralized training restrictions. Moreover, BC-FedHVAE-GRU reached
      ROCAUC 0.957 and PR-AUC 0.943 but federated optimization increased F1
      -score to 0.856 after ten rounds. The interpretability of the framework,
      provided by SHAP and an autonomous policy of governance implemented by a
      DQN, support the transparency of the framework and its independent
      compliance. In general, BC-FedHVAE-GRU will provide a unified,
      privacy-sensitive and audit-capable framework of efficient smart-data
      management in real-time, which is suitable to the regulatory and
      operational demands of next-generation distributed settings. © 2026 IEEE.
