PB  - Insight Society
AB  - Artificial intelligence systems are being increasingly embedded into
      domains where design has been a key pillar, with little guidance from
      designers on how to operationalize trust within design practices. This
      work introduces the Trustworthy HumanCentered AI (THC-AI) design
      conceptual framework, which provides an organized set of considerations
      for designers to embed trustworthiness throughout the design lifecycle. We
      propose a five-step THC-AI design process (Empathy, Define, Ideate,
      Design, and Impact) based on an integrative review of human–computer
      interaction literature on trust and the existing AI governance and
      guidance frameworks, including the EU AI Act, ISO/IEC 24028, NIST AI Risk
      Management Framework, OECD AI Principles, and IEEE Ethically Aligned
      Design. The framework classifies fifteen trustworthiness attributes by
      when to prioritize them during the design process and links them to
      relevant design tools and stakeholders. It emphasizes a design-operational
      perspective on building trustworthy AI by recognizing the temporal and
      path dependency of trustworthiness attributes. We illustrate the
      framework's application in a healthcare clinical decision support use
      case. We also discuss how deferring fairness considerations to later
      design stages may limit downstream system performance in a
      fairness-deferral scenario. The framework's additional contributions
      include an organized classification of fairness notions and guidance in
      choosing individual-, group-, and counterfactual fairness categories for
      designers. Overall, it provides a practical foundation for designers and
      informs future work on empirical evaluations of the framework in
      generative AI and cross-cultural design scenarios. © 2026, IJASEIT is
      licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0
      International License.
