TI  - An AI-interpretable multidimensional intelligence-driven framework for
      student performance prediction using local LLM-based narrative reasoning
T2  - Intell. Data Anal.
JF  - Intelligent Data Analysis
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - SAGE Publications Ltd
AB  - Predicting student performance has become a vital objective in educational
      data mining, however existing models remain limited by limited feature
      sets, opaque decision processes, and the absence of real-time,
      context-aware interpretative intelligence. This study presents a novel
      hybrid Artificial Intelligence (AI) architecture that integrates
      cognitive, emotional, and general intelligence indicators with formative
      assessment/class performance data to construct a multidimensional
      demonstration of student performance. The system utilizes a mathematically
      formalized feature-weighting mechanism that allows domain experts to
      regulate the influence of key predictors prior to training, enabling a
      tunable and educator-configurable modeling process. Interaction between
      cognitive, emotional, and behavioral properties are explicitly engineered
      to capture complex effects often overlooked in prior research. An ensemble
      predictive model combining Random Forests, Gradient Boosting, and Linear
      Regression is used to predict student grades, with uncertainty measured
      through variance-based confidence intervals. The integrated system
      exhibited strong predictive capability, attaining high accuracy
      (R2=0.9014, 95% CI [0.8882, 0.9121], 10,000 bootstrap resamples) with low
      prediction error, confirming the stability and reliability of the ensemble
      model. Understanding why a model produces a particular prediction is often
      as important as the prediction itself. To address the interpretability
      gap, the system incorporates SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP)-based
      global and local explanations, a counterfactual simulation engine that
      estimates the marginal impact of improving specific attributes, and a
      local generative AI layer deployed via Ollama that creates these outputs
      into structured, real-time narrative feedback. Running the Large Language
      Model (LLM) locally ensures privacy-preserving, institution-compliant
      interpretability while permitting interactive, student specific
      explanations. This integration of predictive modeling, explainability,
      simulation, and local generative reasoning creates a unified
      decision-support framework that exceeds traditional black-box approaches.
      Experimental assessment shows that the model not only achieves viable
      predictive accuracy but also provides actionable insights into feature
      influence, model stability, and potential intervention pathways. © The
      Author(s) 2026
