TI  - Data science in ecosystem-service research: A CICES-based bibliometric
      mapping and decision-support synthesis
T2  - J. Environ. Manage.
JF  - Journal of Environmental Management
VL  - 419
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Academic Press
AB  - Data science (DS) methods have attracted growing attention in
      ecosystem-services (ES) research, yet their bibliographic visibility
      across service categories and the implications of this pattern for
      environmental management remain insufficiently understood. This study
      combines bibliometric analysis, CICES-based classification,
      association-rule analysis, and decision-oriented synthesis to examine how
      DS methods are represented across ES domains. Bibliographic records were
      retrieved from the Web of Science Core Collection for 2015–2025. After
      LLM-assisted relevance screening, CICES-based classification, and manual
      validation, 53,227 ES publications were retained. We analyze temporal
      publication trends, geographical and institutional patterns, collaboration
      networks, service-domain distributions, and the bibliographic visibility
      of DS terms in titles, abstracts, author keywords, and Keywords Plus.
      Association-rule analysis was used to identify recurring ES theme–DS
      method associations using appearance, lift, and confidence. The results
      show sustained growth in ES publications and geographically uneven
      research output. DS-related terms were identified in 9.56% of publications
      under the broad bibliographic-field indicator and in 3.17% under the
      keyword-field indicator. Their visibility increased over time but remained
      concentrated in established methods. Random forest and principal component
      analysis were prominent among the highest-scoring theme-method
      associations, whereas deep learning, generative models, and explainable
      artificial intelligence appeared less prominently. The results suggest
      that established DS can support prediction, classification, and
      dimensionality reduction, but effective decision support requires
      validation, uncertainty characterization, interpretability,
      representativeness assessment, and accountability safeguards. The findings
      highlight the need to align ES questions, analytical tasks, data
      structures, validation strategies, management scales, and intended uses
      when designing DS applications for environmental management. © 2026
      Elsevier Ltd
