TI  - Designing evidence-based explainable AI decision support systems: A
      means–end framework for human-centered decision-making
T2  - Technol. Soc.
JF  - Technology in Society
VL  - 90
PY  - 2027
DA  - 2027
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) aims to make the reasoning
      processes of AI models transparent and interpretable, particularly in
      complex decision-making environments. In project-based sectors such as
      construction, where AI-based decision support systems (DSSs) are
      increasingly deployed, limited attention has been given to the role of
      evidence in supporting the reliability, transparency, and accountability
      of their outputs. Without explicitly incorporating evidence into the
      design of XAI-enabled DSSs, the validity of explanations and the
      trustworthiness of system recommendations remain open to question. To
      address this challenge, our paper presents a theoretical, evidence-based
      means–end framework developed through a narrative review. The framework
      provides an epistemic foundation for designing XAI-enabled DSSs that can
      produce Meaningful Human Explanations (MHEs) aligned with end-users'
      knowledge needs and decision-making contexts. It emphasizes evaluating the
      strength, value, and utility of different forms of evidence used to
      support AI-generated explanations. Although the framework is developed
      with construction professionals as its primary end-users, it also applies
      to other stakeholders, including developers, regulators, and project
      managers, whose epistemic goals may differ. By explicitly integrating
      evidence into the design of XAI-enabled DSSs, the framework provides a
      conceptual basis for evidence-based, transparent, and accountable
      AI-supported decision-making. Given its theoretical foundation and broad
      applicability, it can inform the design of evidence-aware XAI systems
      across a range of engineering and industrial domains. © 2026 The Authors.
