TI  - Explainable AI for Risk Assessment and Decision Support
T2  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence: Bridging Concepts, Applications, and
      Future Challenges
SP  - 601-626
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - wiley
AB  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) evolves upon the concepts of
      transparency and interpretability that form the basics of risk assessment
      and decision support supported by AI. Commonly applied AI systems operate
      as “black boxes” which does not help the stakeholders’ trust and the
      verifying of the automated risk evaluations. In question, this chapter
      studies the role of XAI technology in overcoming these difficulties by
      providing a human-readable method to handle complex AI-driven decisions.
      The section starts by presenting the central XAI methods SHAP, LIME,
      decision trees, and counterfactual explanations as well as the
      applications of these in diverse areas of risk, such as finance,
      healthcare, cybersecurity, and supply chain management. We also take a
      look at various ethical challenges like algorithmic bias, fairness,
      regulatory compliance, and the issue of what is better - interpretability
      or accuracy. We offer real-world case studies to illustrate the explicit
      models’ potential to reduce the uncertainties in making informed
      decisions, accountability, and trust in high-risk scenarios. Last but not
      the least on the agenda are futuristic XAI topics like neuro-symbolic AI,
      the emergence of regulatory frameworks, and human-AI interaction as well.
      The overarching message of the chapter is that to reach the transparency
      level in the domain of risk assessment, learn from experience and share
      plans among stakeholders are very important steps for the researchers,
      technology, and business decision makers in the industry. © 2026 Scrivener
      Publishing LLC.
