TI  - Multimodal Vision-Language Models for High- Stakes Decision Making
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Application of multimodal vision-language models (VLMs) has proven to be
      capable of impressive perception, reasoning, and post-discovery abilities;
      however, their use in high-stakes decision-making setups has been limited
      in regard to concerns about reliability, hallucination, as well as
      accountability. This paper introduces a risk-conscious procedure of
      trustworthy multimodal decision-making fitting evidence grounding,
      uncertainty quantification, and selective prediction into one remarkable
      structure. The proposed system makes the models explain the output using
      verifiable visual and textual indicators and simultaneously estimate
      confidence to deployment risk. A counterfactual validation mechanism is
      yet another method that judges how predictions do in cases of
      perturbations, allowing an automatic increase in prediction to human
      experts when the ambiguity goes beyond tolerable limits. Experiments in
      safety-critical situations demonstrate that unsupported claims are
      significantly reduced, and calibration is improved compared to standard
      VLM pipelines and makes them much more robust. These findings provide a
      viable road map to verifiable and human conformable artificial
      intelligence applicable in activities of actual world applications where
      mistakes have enormous societal, legal and economic implications. © 2026
      IEEE.
