TI  - Integrating Artificial Intelligence and Blockchain-enabled IoT for
      sustainable supply chain practices
T2  - Artificial Intelligence for Sustainable Supply Chain Management
SP  - 42-61
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - CRC Press
AB  - Integrating blockchain-enabled IoT (BIoT) and artificial intelligence (AI)
      technologies in supply chain management (SCM) has provided transparency,
      automation, and sustainability opportunities towards discovering SCM
      redefinition. Concerning supply chain visibility, resilience, and ethical
      intertwinement, this chapter presents the advanced architecture of the
      cognitive blockchain–IoT–AI supply chain orchestration framework that
      associates blockchain-based immutable data validation with AI-based
      predictive analytics and IoT-based real-time monitoring. The proposed
      layered approach collects sensor data from IoT devices. It uses edge-AI
      algorithms to detect anomalies, and smart contracts use the blockchain
      ledger to commit the validated results. Explainable AI (XAI) methods make
      decision-making interpretable and trustworthy, while federated learning
      allows decentralised, privacy-preserving model training across distributed
      nodes. Due to limitations in the traditional rules-based approach,
      empirical validation over 90 days of simulated cold chain logistics
      datasets may confirm a significant improvement in detection accuracy,
      knowledge drift adaptation, and an operational walkability factor. This
      work encompasses cross-domain applications in several industries such as
      manufacturing, medicine, and agriculture; grounding theory; and actual
      implementation techniques. To ensure social alignment, the design includes
      ethical vectors such as sustainability indicators, ethical AI
      methodologies, and data governance laws. The chapter offers a complete
      roadmap for crafting innovative, sustainable, and future-ready supply
      chains using BIoT and AI technologies. © 2026 selection and editorial
      matter, N. Nasurudeen Ahamed, P. Karthikeyan and Polinpapilinho F. Katina;
      individual chapters, the contributors.
