TI  - Modernizing the creation of enlisted qualification standards for the
      United States Air Force
T2  - Comput. Ind. Eng.
JF  - Computers & Industrial Engineering
VL  - 221
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Career Field Managers (CFMs) in the United States Air Force (USAF) set
      qualification standards for entry into enlisted career fields,
      traditionally with the aid of Air Force Personnel Center and its
      application of classical statistical models. However, in view of recent
      federal guidance emphasizing responsible artificial intelligence (RAI),
      this research explores the modernization of the standard-setting process
      via interpretable and explainable machine learning methods. We develop
      alternative predictive success models for the enlisted targeting analyst
      career field, expressly addressing the RAI principles of responsibility,
      equitability, traceability, reliability, and governability. Our
      methodology includes the construction and testing of performance-focused,
      transparency-focused, and error-parity-focused classifiers, leveraging
      modern ML methods to facilitate trust and accountability. Benchmarking
      these against existent baselines reveals substantive quantitative
      improvements. Specifically, under select imputation schemes, the
      performance-focused models increased predictive accuracy by up to 19% and
      F1-scores by up to 13%, whereas the error-parity-focused models were able
      to assure adherence to federal employment law with limited degradation to
      predictive power. We also create a prototype decision support system (DSS)
      that empowers CFMs to develop qualification standards through a graphical
      user interface, streamlining operations and enhancing decision-making.
      This DSS facilitates responsible ML model selection, supports better
      corporate placement of recruits, provides insights for recruiters, and
      ensures adherence to federal policy and law. Our results demonstrate the
      feasibility of modernizing USAF career field standards via responsible
      machine learning, highlighting the tradeoffs between model performance,
      interpretability, and fairness. © 2026
