TI  - Explainable AI for Chronic Kidney Disease Prediction in Medical IoT: A
      Systematic Review
SP  - 692-699
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) is a significant health problem worldwide
      which impacts millions of people and can develop without symptoms until
      late stages of the disease, thus early diagnosis and prediction of the
      disease is critical to improve patient outcomes. The medical Internet of
      Things (MIoT) and the need for accurate, trustworthy, and transparent
      clinical decision-support systems have highlighted the role of Explainable
      Artificial Intelligence (XAI) in the prediction of CKD in these
      environments. This systematic review aims to explore the role of
      Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) in the prediction of CKD in
      Medical Internet of Things (MIoT) environments. The review systematically
      examines the recent literature to understand how to incorporate the use of
      health monitoring devices with machine learning, deep learning and
      explainable AI approaches in the diagnosis, risk assessment, and
      prediction of CKD. The literature was assessed for the type of data
      sources, predictive models, explainability techniques, performance
      metrics, and clinical application. The results show that, despite having
      high diagnostic accuracy, the models, when enhanced by XAI, such as SHAP,
      LIME, attention-based neural networks, and rule-based explainability
      frameworks, significantly boost the interpretability of CKD predictions.
      For several studies, the prediction accuracy was above 90%, and they were
      more trusted and transparent than traditional blackbox models, and allowed
      to make better decisions. Moreover, by using the MIoT technology,
      continuous monitoring of patients allows them to assess their health in
      real-time and detect any abnormalities in the kidneys. The review
      identifies and underscores current challenges such as data privacy, model
      generalization, interoperability and regulatory compliance, and provides
      future research directions. Overall, the integration between XAI and MIoT
      holds the potential for building trustworthy, patient-centric, and
      clinically actionable CKD prediction models that could revolutionize
      personalized care and enhance the long-term management of the disease. ©
      2026 IEEE.
