TI  - An Adaptive Federated Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Explainable Deep
      Learning and Smart Decision-Making in Healthcare IoT Using MIMIC-IV
SP  - 561-566
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The fast growth of Internet of Things (IoT) in healthcare has allowed
      real-time patient data monitoring, but issues of data privacy,
      explainability, and adaptive decision-making are not yet resolved. This
      paper proposes AFNS-IoT, an Adaptive Federated Neuro-Symbolic IoT
      framework that integrates deep learning, neuro-symbolic reasoning, and
      reinforcement learning for intelligent and explainable healthcare
      decision-making. Leveraging the MIMIC-IV dataset, local IoT edge nodes are
      trained using federated deep learning models to preserve patient privacy,
      while a multi-objective weighted aggregation balances accuracy, latency,
      and device constraints. A neuro-symbolic fusion layer translates model
      outputs into interpretable symbolic rules, providing transparent decision
      pathways for clinical decision support. Furthermore, a reinforcement
      learning feedback loop adaptively refines the system based on real-world
      outcomes. Experimental results demonstrate that AFNS-IoT achieves the
      highest accuracy (0.94), F1-score (0.92), and AUC (0.95), outperforming
      all baseline models. Furthermore, it reduces inference latency to 140 ms,
      compared to 220 ms in centralized learning, while achieving superior
      energy efficiency (0.88). Unlike conventional deep learning and federated
      approaches that lack interpretability, AFNS-IoT provides very high
      interpretability fidelity through its neuro-symbolic fusion layer. The
      findings demonstrate how AFNS-IoT can be used as the next-generation
      framework of privacy, explainable and adaptive healthcare IoT systems. ©
      2026 IEEE.
