TI  - From accurate AI to sustainable AI: A lifecycle computational framework
      for resource-efficient, explainable, and trustworthy artificial
      intelligence in energy systems
T2  - J. Comput. Sci.
JF  - Journal of Computational Science
VL  - 102
PY  - 2027
DA  - 2027
PB  - Elsevier B.V.
AB  - Artificial intelligence is increasingly used to improve forecasting,
      optimization, control, and decision support in energy systems, yet much of
      the literature remains dominated by accuracy-centric evaluation. This
      critical review argues that such evaluation is insufficient for
      sustainable energy transitions because AI models also impose
      computational, environmental, operational, and governance costs across
      their lifecycle. Rather than descriptively summarizing prior work, the
      paper comparatively examines major groups of studies, including AI for
      renewable-energy forecasting, smart-grid optimization, energy management,
      Green AI, explainable AI, and trustworthy AI, to identify where their
      assumptions, metrics, and deployment priorities converge or conflict. The
      review shows that existing research often treats model performance,
      computational efficiency, carbon footprint, explainability, robustness,
      privacy, and accountability as separate concerns, leaving a gap in
      integrated lifecycle assessment. To address this gap, the paper proposes a
      lifecycle computational framework for sustainable AI in energy systems,
      covering data acquisition, model design, training, inference, deployment,
      monitoring, retraining, and governance. The framework supports
      multi-dimensional evaluation of AI systems in terms of accuracy, resource
      efficiency, carbon impact, transparency, reliability, and real-world
      deployability, offering a pathway from merely accurate AI toward
      sustainable, explainable, and trustworthy AI for energy applications. The
      Authors This is an open access article under the CC BY license.
      http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
