TI  - Hybrid human–AI decision-making for fire-related disaster risk reduction
T2  - Prog. Disaster Sci.
JF  - Progress in Disaster Science
VL  - 30
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Advances in sensing technologies and machine learning are increasingly
      influencing fire safety practices in critical transport infrastructures.
      At the same time, fire-related emergencies remain characterized by
      uncertainty, time pressure, cognitive overload, and public accountability,
      particularly in technologically complex and densely populated
      environments. From a disaster risk reduction (DRR) perspective, these
      conditions expose the limitations of fully automated or purely
      human-centered decision-making approaches. This study proposes a hybrid
      Human–AI Decision Loop for fire risk management that integrates
      uncertainty quantification (UQ), explainable artificial intelligence
      (XAI), and calibrated human oversight as a mechanism for reducing the
      impact of cascading disasters. A structured scoping review of
      peer-reviewed studies and authoritative institutional reports published
      between 2018 and 2025 was conducted across building, industrial, wildland,
      and transport contexts. The review synthesizes how AI systems contribute
      to detection, prediction, and decision support, while human operators
      provide contextual sensemaking, ethical judgment, and accountability under
      uncertainty. Based on this synthesis, a six-stage decision framework—Sense
      → Predict → Explain → Human Gate → Act → Learn—is articulated, capturing
      the dynamic interaction between algorithmic inference and human control.
      Key operational requirements are identified, including uncertainty
      visualization, rationale communication, alert triage, and structured
      override logging, alongside evaluation indicators such as false alarm
      rates, response latency, situational awareness, and trust calibration. The
      proposed framework contributes to disaster risk reduction by offering a
      transferable decision architecture for fire-related emergencies in
      transport and other critical infrastructures, providing a
      governance-aligned foundation for implementing hybrid decision
      architectures in safety-critical transport environments. © 2026 The
      Author.
