TI  - Enhancing Organizational Decision-Making through Explainable AI (XAI)
      Capabilities: A Systematic Literature Review
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The growing adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in organizational and
      tactical decision-making has intensified concerns about transparency,
      accountability and the changing role of human decision-making in high-risk
      decision environments. Explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) has
      developed as a prominent approach for improving the explainability of
      AI-assisted decisions, however existing research remains fragmented across
      technical explainability, user reliance and moral governance perspectives.
      To address this gap, this study conducts a systematic literature review of
      28 peer-reviewed studies published between 2020 and 2026 to examine how
      XAI is conceptualized and applied within organizational decision-making
      contexts from a human-centric perspective. Using the PRISMA framework and
      inductive thematic synthesis, the findings indicate that XAI is
      predominantly positioned as a transparency-and explanation-oriented
      decision-support capability that supports human sensemaking, oversight,
      and calibrated trust rather than autonomous decision-making. The reviewed
      literature primarily focuses on managerial decision contexts, while
      strategic governance, board-level decision-making and organizational
      accountability remain relatively underexplored. This study adds to the
      literature by developing a conceptually based Human-Centric XAI Maturity
      Framework that explains how explainability progressively evolves from
      interpretative decision support into an institutionally embedded
      organizational governance capability. © 2026 IEEE.
