TI  - Dropout risk in higher education via multidimensional features and machine
      learning
SP  - 25-30
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery, Inc
AB  - Student turnover is a major challenge for higher education institutions.
      Early identification of high dropout risk students is of great
      significance for improving retention rate and optimizing resource
      allocation. Based on multi-source educational data, this study constructs
      a machine learning prediction framework, aiming to accurately classify
      students into two categories:’ dropout’ and’ non-dropout’. By mining data
      sets containing multi-dimensional features such as demographic
      characteristics, socioeconomic status, and academic performance, various
      algorithms such as random forest, XGBoost, support vector machine,
      logistic regression, and stacked ensemble are compared. The results show
      that the ensemble learning method performs better in this prediction task.
      In addition, SHAP technology is used to analyze the attribution of model
      output. The experimental results show that the proposed model has
      advantages in F1 score and recall rate. The analysis of feature importance
      further reveals the core factors affecting students’ dropout, such as
      course pass rate and tuition payment status. The above findings provide
      highly interpretable decision support for education administrators,
      thereby enhancing the credibility and transparency of artificial
      intelligence in educational applications. © 2026 Copyright held by the
      owner/author(s).
