TI  - A data-driven decision support framework for smart campus governance:
      Integrating heterogeneous educational data sources with optical
      technologies
VL  - 14064
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - SPIE
AB  - The rapid digital transformation of educational institutions has led to
      the proliferation of heterogeneous data sources in smart campuses,
      including academic records, IoT sensor data, optical attendance logs, and
      behavioral logs. However, the lack of an integrated decision-support
      system hinders administrators from leveraging these diverse datasets for
      evidence-based governance. Existing solutions often suffer from static
      data integration models, limited scalability, and insufficient
      transparency in decision-making processes. To address these challenges,
      this study proposes a Data-Driven Decision Support Framework for Smart
      Campus Governance (DDSF-SC), which enables real-Time analysis and
      actionable insights from multi-modal educational data enhanced by optical
      character recognition for document processing and fiber-optic backbones
      for low-latency data transmission. The framework adopts a microservices
      architecture to ensure modularity and scalability, incorporating a
      federated learning mechanism to preserve data privacy. It introduces a
      dynamic weighting model for multidimensional assessment, along with edge
      computing components to reduce latency in real-Time analytics. A key
      innovation is the development of plug-And-play data adapters that
      standardize heterogeneous inputs from learning management systems, IoT
      devices, optical attendance systems, and administrative databases. The
      implementation leverages Kubernetes for orchestration, Apache Kafka for
      stream processing, and explainable AI techniques to enhance decision
      interpretability. Evaluation at a university pilot demonstrates a 32%
      improvement in operational efficiency and a 41% reduction in decision
      response time compared to traditional methods. The framework facilitates
      data-driven policy adjustments in resource allocation, curriculum
      planning, and infrastructure management. The findings provide actionable
      insights for institutions transitioning toward smart campus ecosystems
      while balancing innovation with regulatory compliance. © COPYRIGHT SPIE.
      Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.
