TI  - Transparent artificial intelligence for treatment planning
T2  - Explainable AI in Clinical Practice: Methods, Applications, and
      Implementation
SP  - 373-392
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) is used more in healthcare to help doctors
      give better advice on how patients can be treated. AI, however, is a
      “black box” and many people have raised questions about ethics,
      responsibility, and trust. Transparent AI in treatment planning helps and
      allows patients, healthcare providers to build trust with the AI-generated
      recommendations by making them more comprehensible, interpretable, and in
      line with clinical best practices. The challenges of biased treatment
      recommendations, ethical and legal concerns, and clinical mistrust
      associated with AI-based decision-making can only be addressed by
      transparency in AI-driven treatment planning. Examples of Explainable AI
      strategies, like model-agnostic approaches such as SHAP and LIME,
      rule-based systems, and feature importance analysis, are important to help
      understand AI models. The use of human-AI collaboration in decision
      support systems, intuitive user interfaces, and clinician training is
      another way to increase the level of transparent AI in clinical
      operations. When we use AI for treating patients, we need to be careful
      about the rules. Our plans must follow HIPAA, GDPR, and the FDA’s
      suggestions. Case studies from the fields of psychiatry, cardiovascular
      care, and oncology indicate that transparent AI models can improve patient
      outcomes without sacrificing interpretability. However, there are still
      challenges, such as complex clinical workflows, data privacy, and the
      trade-off between explainability and accuracy. Future research should
      focus on building transparent and high-performance hybrid AI models,
      improving the explainability of black-box models, increasing patient
      involvement, and standardizing regulatory frameworks. For trustworthy,
      ethical, and patient-centric healthcare solutions—and ultimately more
      effective clinical decisions and treatments—transparent AI in treatment
      planning is crucial. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
