TI  - A Systematic Review Federated and Explainable AI Framework for
      Privacy-Preserving Diagnosis of Sleep Disorders Using Multimodal
      Physiological Data
VL  - 2052 LNNS
SP  - 12-23
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - The sleep disorders remain one of the most undiagnosed and critical issues
      across the world in regard to their effects on the cognitive abilities,
      emotional stability as well as the physiological well-being.
      Polysomnography is a clinical gold standard using a laboratory method of
      diagnosis of sleep disorders; however, its high cost, complex operation,
      and inability to perform at large scale or longitudinal screening are
      limiting in its application. Recent developments in artificial
      intelligence have shown promising prospects of automation of sleep with
      multimodal physiological measures, such as electroencephalography and
      electrocardiography. Nevertheless, the majority of the current AI-based
      diagnostic systems are based on centralized data aggregation, which
      creates serious concerns with regard to the concepts of data privacy,
      regulatory compliance, interpretability, and inter-institutional
      generalizability. The review methodically reviews the recent papers on the
      topic of federated learning, and sleep disorder diagnosis. Federated
      learning is considered as a privacy-sensitive paradigm that allows joint
      model training between distributed healthcare organizations without
      exposing raw patient data, which overcomes the limitation of governance
      and regulations. Explainable AI methods such as SHAP and LIME are
      discussed as to how they can improve clinical transparency, trust and
      accountability in automated diagnostic systems. © The Author(s), under
      exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
