TI  - Privacy preserving and explainable fetal health prediction from
      cardiotocography using synthetic data and transformer-based federated
      learning
T2  - Biomed. Signal Process. Control
JF  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 128
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier Ltd
AB  - Privacy-preserving and explainable machine learning for fetal health
      prediction is essential for trustworthy clinical decision support under
      strict medical data regulations. This study proposes a unified framework
      for fetal health classification from cardiotocography (CTG) data,
      integrating synthetic data generation, a transformer-based feature
      interaction module, federated learning, differential privacy, and
      explainable artificial intelligence. To address data scarcity and class
      imbalance, a hybrid synthetic data pipeline is developed using variational
      autoencoders (VAEs), conditional tabular GANs (CTGANs), and a large
      language model (LLM)-based generation. To improve structured data
      modeling, an Inter-Feature Attention Transformer (IFAT) is introduced that
      treats each clinical variable as a token and captures inter-feature
      dependencies via self-attention. In decentralized healthcare settings,
      federated learning enables collaborative training without sharing raw data
      and is evaluated under IID and non-IID settings to study statistical
      heterogeneity in clinical distributions. Differentially Private Stochastic
      Gradient Descent (DP-SGD) is employed during optimization to provide
      formal privacy guarantees (ϵ≈3.0, δ=10−5), maintaining 92%–93%
      classification accuracy while preserving the privacy of individual
      training samples. SHAP-based explainability ensures interpretability
      across centralized, federated, and privacy-preserving settings,
      consistently identifying fetal heart rate, short-term variability, and
      accelerations as key predictors. The proposed framework exhibits linear
      computational complexity with respect to dataset size, feature dimension,
      model parameters, and the number of participating clients, making it
      suitable for large-scale privacy-preserving healthcare deployments.
      Experimental results show strong fetal health classification performance
      under multiple privacy-preserving configurations, with synthetic
      augmentation and IFAT achieving up to 98% accuracy, an Average Precision
      (AP) score of 0.99, an F1-score of 0.98, and an ROC-AUC of 0.99,
      outperforming baseline methods. The proposed framework establishes a
      scalable, privacy-aware, and interpretable AI system for secure fetal
      health prediction in real-world healthcare systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
