TI  - A Dual-Purpose Interpretable Ensemble Framework of Fraud Stratification
      and Credit Risk
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Modern financial institutions are under greater pressure to not only
      identify a fraudulent action but also to generate transparent credit
      evaluation, and one in which the regulation is not compromised. Modern
      pipelines generally approach these as discrete tasks which results in
      disjointed attribute mapping and black box behaviours not meeting the
      regulatory requirement of traceability. In this paper, the authors propose
      a single framework (called Dual-Objective Interpretable Ensemble) that
      simultaneously fraud stratifies and scores credit using a single
      interpretable framework. DOIE combines a diversity-optimal ensemble
      backbone with a hierarchical say layer, resorting to monotonicity based
      constrained learners, trajectory union between local and global
      attribution, as well as sample boundary singleness characterization. The
      outputs of the system are synchronized: a categorical risk of fraud-risk
      and a continuous credit rating and a report to the regulator consolidating
      rule-level evidence. The methodology has the benefit of providing every
      judgment with an equivalent and evidenced analytical rationale.
      Experimental analysis of multimodal financial data shows that DOIE is able
      to reach an F1-score of 0.962 with an association rate of 0.011 on fraud
      detection, as well as an AUC of 0.947 on credit default prediction.
      Besides, the framework retains over 0.91 accountability scores in all
      elements. These findings confirm that DOIE is a powerful framework that
      can reconcile high-performance predictive analytics with the demands of
      financial compliance that are highly interpretable. © 2026 IEEE.
