TI  - CareFusion AI: Evidence-Based Agentic Intelligence for Multi-Modal
      Clinical Decision Support
SP  - 653-658
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Clinical decision support systems increasingly rely on large language
      models and deep learning; however, existing approaches often suffer from
      hallucination, limited explainability, and systematic bias toward common
      diseases. These limitations are particularly critical in safety-sensitive
      medical settings, where unverifiable predictions and rare disease
      suppression can lead to severe clinical consequences. This paper presents
      CareFusion AI, an evidence-based, agentic, multi-modal clinical decision
      support framework designed to operate in a local-first and
      privacy-preserving environment. The system integrates four complementary
      intelligence modules: (i) symptom-based reasoning using closed-vocabulary
      extraction and hierarchical bucketed anomaly detection, (ii) explainable
      medical image analysis with enforced spatial validity, (iii) deterministic
      genomic variant interpretation grounded in ClinVar evidence, and (iv)
      temporal risk trajectory modeling using longitudinal patient data. To
      prevent unsupported inference, all generative reasoning is explicitly
      constrained by retrieved clinical evidence through a verification-oriented
      retrieval-augmented generation pipeline. The proposed architecture
      emphasizes transparency, auditability, and conservative decision-making
      rather than autonomous diagnosis. Experimental evaluation on public and
      anonymized datasets, including MIMIC-IV, NIH Chest X-ray, and ClinVar,
      demonstrates strong diagnostic coverage, low hallucination rates, and
      clinically meaningful explainability across modalities. These results
      indicate that CareFusion AI provides a robust foundation for safe,
      multi-modal clinical decision support in research and resource-constrained
      environments. © 2026 IEEE.
