TI  - A Unified Cloud-Native Artificial Intelligence Platform for End-to-End
      Insurance Risk, Pricing, and Claims Analytics
SP  - 100-107
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Artificial intelligence is increasingly used in insurance to support
      underwriting, premium pricing, claims management, fraud detection, and
      catastrophe risk modeling. Although machine learning methods have shown
      strong predictive performance in these areas, many deployments remain
      fragmented, with models and data pipelines implemented as isolated
      components and limited support for explainability, monitoring, and
      operational governance. This fragmentation reduces consistency,
      complicates regulatory review, and slows integration into production
      decision workflows. This paper presents a Unified Insurance AI Platform
      (UIAP), a cloud-native architecture that supports end-to-end insurance
      analytics within a single operational framework. UIAP integrates
      multi-source data ingestion, centralized feature management, model
      development and deployment, explainability services, performance
      monitoring, and business-facing decision dashboards. The platform supports
      both batch and real-time inference through a shared pipeline with built-in
      governance controls and auditability features. A case study based on
      representative insurance analytics workflows is used to evaluate the
      platform, demonstrating improved operational efficiency, consistent model
      management, and increased transparency in decision support. The proposed
      platform provides a practical blueprint for scalable and governance-aware
      insurance AI systems.Impact Statement - Property and casualty insurance
      organizations increasingly rely on advanced analytics to support
      underwriting, pricing, claims management, fraud detection, and catastrophe
      risk assessment. However, many insurers continue to operate fragmented
      analytical systems that limit scalability, transparency, and timely
      decision support. The unified insurance AI platform presented in this
      paper illustrates how cloud-native architectures and integrated
      operational practices can consolidate multiple insurance analytics
      functions within a single environment. By unifying data ingestion, model
      lifecycle management, explainability, and decision dashboards, the
      platform supports improved operational efficiency, analytical consistency,
      and governance across insurance workflows. Evaluation results indicate
      favorable performance characteristics, reduced operational complexity, and
      increased transparency in high-impact insurance decisions under
      representative conditions. Beyond insurance, the architectural principles
      described in this work provide a transferable reference for deploying
      scalable and governance-aware AI systems in other regulated financial
      services domains where auditability and oversight are essential. © 2026
      IEEE.
