TI  - Risk Management in Series Algorithm Development and Deployment: A Medical
      Risk Management Perspective
T2  - Algorithm Management Optimization: Intelligent Series Algorithm Management
      in Healthcare
SP  - 114-131
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Bentham Science Publishers
AB  - Like any other sector, algorithm application in healthcare also needs
      well-founded risk management policies to apply in the medical technology
      context. Data processing in series algorithms, executed sequentially, is
      associated with the following challenges: system integrity, accuracy,
      ethics, and compliance. What is even worse, these issues are exacerbated
      by the fact that medical settings are highly sensitive, and mistakes in
      predictions or biased results can have disastrous results on patients.
      This paper identifies the key threats to the creation and implementation
      of series algorithms in medicine and proposes a feasible solution to
      address them. A detailed approach is developed based on the evaluation of
      actual case scenarios to enhance the execution of medical algorithms in
      terms of risk, safety, reliability, and ethics. Among the key areas of
      focus are data validation, bias mitigation, transparency, and algorithmic
      self-monitoring. Furthermore, this study examines the increasing trend of
      AI regulation and its implications for implementing AI in horizontal
      sectors such as medicine, where risk management arrangements must change.
      Finally, the paper recommends a partnership among data scientists,
      physicians, and healthcare policymakers, as an interdisciplinary approach
      is needed to ensure the safety and efficiency of series algorithms,
      particularly in patient outcomes. © 2026 Bentham Science Publishers.
