VL  - 32
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier B.V.
AB  - Background: Translating data-driven AI innovations into clinically
      meaningful solutions requires addressing biomedical data complexity and
      the need for explainable models. We introduce a novel approach to
      personalized medicine by leveraging Dynamic Digital Twins (DDT) to
      represent real-time patient states. Methods: This study proposes an
      explainable, multimodal architecture combining 1D physiological signals
      (EEG arrays for cognitive state) and 2D medical imaging (wireless capsule
      endoscopy). Utilizing a Graph Attention Network (GAT) integrated with a
      federated learning framework, the system processes cross-modal features
      while preserving data privacy across distributed clinical nodes. Results:
      In rigorous clinical validation protocols, the multimodal DDT model
      achieved a diagnostic accuracy of 94.8% (95% CI: 93.1%–96.4%),
      representing a 14.2% improvement over unimodal baselines. Processing
      latency was reduced by 31%, and clinician-assessed trustworthiness
      improved significantly, with SHAP-based interpretability scores reaching
      0.89/1.0. Conclusion: By successfully synthesizing cognitive biomarkers
      and localized imaging into a unified digital twin, this framework provides
      a highly accurate, transparent, and privacy-preserving tool ready for
      real-world clinical implementation. © 2026 The Author
