TI  - A Blockchain-Governed Clinical Decision Support Framework with Federated
      Learning and Explainability
T2  - Int. J. Adv. Comput. Sci. Appl.
JF  - International Journal of Advanced Computer Science and Applications
VL  - 17
IS  - 10
SP  - 1455-1469
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Science and Information Organization
AB  - Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) are widely used to support
      diagnosis, risk prediction, and treatment planning. However, many existing
      systems are limited by institution-centric data handling, weak
      auditability, and a lack of clear explanations at the point of clinical
      decision-making. Although blockchain-based federated learning approaches
      improve the security of model exchange, they often treat consent
      management, autonomous reasoning, model drift handling, and
      clinician-focused explainability as separate components. To address this
      gap, this study proposes CDSS-AgentXAI, a blockchainenhanced agentic AI
      framework for multi-institutional clinical decision support. The framework
      combines smart contract-based consent control, privacy-preserving
      federated learning, decisiontime explainable inference, and drift-aware
      model adaptation within one auditable pipeline. It specifies a
      permissioned Hyperledger Fabric layer for consent and audit tracking,
      FedProx aggregation with differentially private updates, and an agentic
      decision module that combines reinforcement learning with a neural
      network. Clinician-facing explanations are specified using SHAP, LIME, and
      counterfactual reasoning, and ADWIN-based drift monitoring is specified to
      trigger feedback-driven retraining. The federated learning and privacy
      layer was evaluated on 67,989 ICU stays from MIMIC-IV v3.1 across three
      simulated hospital nodes, with seven configurations compared over five
      random seeds. Test AUC-ROC ranged from 0.883 to 0.896, and FedProx with
      differential privacy (0.893 ± 0.003) did not differ significantly from the
      non-private and centralized models. © (2026), (Science and Information
      Organization). All rights reserved.
