TI  - Lightweight XAI 2.0 for Edge Devices to ensure Real-Time Monitoring in
      Healthcare 4.0
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The use of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare has led to important
      advances in clinical decision support, patient monitoring, and medical
      diagnosis. But, in vital clinical contexts where understanding and
      transparency are important, the complicated nature of AI models can
      sometimes prevent their acceptance. Explainable AI (XAI) strategies,
      particularly SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP), are popular for their
      ability to relate model predictions to input features, which supports more
      accountability and more confidence. Sometimes, traditional approaches to
      SHAP can be resource-inefficient and require a lot of memory and
      computation, which limits the realistic use of SHAP on healthcare edge
      devices that may have limited physical resources, like wearable monitors,
      portable diagnostic devices, and sensors powered by Internet of Things.
      Due to this, we introduce a more lightweight, resource-efficient SHAP
      framework intended for use in healthcare edge contexts that would provide
      real-time interpretability and mitigate the need for performance
      degradation. The proposed method leverages hardware-aware optimization,
      feature-space diversity selection, and background data reduction to reduce
      computational complexity, while preserving high fidelity in explanations
      of prediction. The explanation resolution is adaptively adjusted
      dynamically based on clinical urgency, latency constraints, and model
      confidence using an adaptive compression mechanism. Compared to standard
      SHAP implementations, experimental results indicate that a lightweight
      SHAP can achieve latency, energy, and memory savings of up to 60 - 80%
      while retaining over 90% correlation with full explanation. The proposed
      framework enables transparent, trusted, and practical AI implementation at
      the healthcare edge for real-time, interpretable medical intelligence in
      real-world clinical contexts. © 2026 IEEE.
