AU  - Saragih, D S
AU  - Panduwiyasa, H
AU  - Febriyani, W
AD  - Telkom University, Department of Information System, Bandung, Indonesia
TI  - User Adoption of an AI-Enabled E-Counseling Platform in West Java: A DOI
      Approach with Privacy-Security Trust Mediation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - AI-enabled e-counseling platforms introduce unique technical architectural
      challenges, including NLP models, predictive analytics, and API-layer
      security protocols, that distinguish them from conventional digital health
      services and create adoption barriers related to user trust. This study
      investigates how user perceptions of Data Privacy (PD) and System Security
      (KS) as a 'Privacy-Security Trust' mediation mechanism relate to key
      Diffusion of Innovation (DOI) attribute perceptions, Relative Advantage
      (RA), Compatibility (CO), Complexity (CX), Trialability (TR), and
      Observability (OB), among users in West Java, Indonesia. DOI was selected
      over UTAUT and TAM because its innovation-attribute perspective directly
      maps onto the novelty evaluation process inherent in first-time adoption
      of AI-enabled mental health services. A cross-sectional online survey of
      67 valid respondents was analyzed using SEM-PLS in SmartPLS 4. The
      measurement model demonstrates strong psychometric properties (outer
      loadings 0.789-0.971, AVE 0.743-0.827, Cronbach's alpha 0.904-0.933,
      composite reliability 0.933-0.950). The structural model shows substantial
      explanatory power for OB R2=0.781) and moderate power for TR R2=0.675),
      whereas CO, CX, and RA exhibit weaker explanatory power, partly due to
      unmeasured factors such as prior digital health experience. PD positively
      influences OB (β=0.639), RA (β= 0.488), and TR (β=0.616), and KS
      positively influences CX (β=0. 497), while other paths are not supported.
      These findings suggest that privacy assurance through Privacy-Preserving
      Machine Learning (PPML) can enhance value- and experience-related
      evaluations, while security implementation incorporating Explainable AI
      (XAI) modules should minimize perceived complexity in AI-enabled
      e-counseling services. © 2026 IEEE.
