AB  - Deep learning (DL) has revolutionised medical data analysis by enabling
      automated, high precision diagnosis, predictive analytics and personalised
      medicine. Traditional medical data processing methods often struggle with
      complex and high-dimensional datasets while DL models can efficiently
      extract patterns and insights to enhance healthcare outcomes.
      Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have achieved impressive success in
      medical image analysis, performing at expert levels in applications like
      tumour detection, fracture identification and organ segmentation.
      Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) and long short-term memory (LSTM)
      networks have proven effective in processing sequential medical data like
      electrocardiograms (ECGs) and electroencephalograms (EEGs). Transformer
      models like BERT and Vision Transformers (ViTs) are leading the way in
      medical text processing and image interpretation. This enhances the
      precision of clinical decision-making. Generative adversarial networks
      (GANs) and autoencoders have also been used for medical image enhancement
      and synthetic data creation to overcome data shortage challenges. In
      addition, graph neural networks (GNNs) are capable of drug discovery and
      biomedical network analysis to augment precision medicine strategies.
      Despite these developments, issues like interpretability, data privacy and
      regulatory compliance continue to be major obstacles to their adoption.
      The use of federated learning (FL) and explainable artificial intelligence
      (AI XAI) methods is being researched to solve these problems while
      maintaining the transparency and robustness of the models in clinical
      environments. Future research opportunities include DL integration with
      Internet of Medical Things (IoMT), multi-modal learning for comprehensive
      patient analysis, and real-time decision support systems. This chapter
      discusses the application of DL in medicine, highlighting different model
      architectures and their influence on different categories of medical data,
      such as structured electronic health records (EHRs), unstructured clinical
      notes, medical imaging, genomic sequences, and real-time physiological
      signals. And their applications, challenges, and future potential. With
      the use of artificial intelligence-driven (AI-driven) insights, DL has the
      potential to transform diagnostics, treatment planning, and global
      healthcare systems, ultimately enhancing patient outcomes. © 2027
      selection and editorial matter, Vijay Jeyakumar, N. Venkateswaran and
      Dinesh Bhatia.
