TI  - A counterintuitive approach to explainable AI in healthcare: balancing
      transparency, efficiency, and cost
T2  - AI Soc.
JF  - AI & Society
VL  - 40
IS  - 7
SP  - 5735-5741
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) is increasingly championed for
      its potential to enhance transparency and accountability in clinical
      decision support. However, emerging evidence reveals a critical trade-off:
      these benefits may not come without significant costs. While deep neural
      networks achieve remarkable accuracy in tasks like diabetic retinopathy
      diagnosis (AUROCs near 0.91) (Aziz et al. in medRxiv
      https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.08.10.24311735, 2024), the pursuit of
      interpretability often necessitates simpler models, leading to a notable
      performance drop (AUROCs of 0.78–0.82), potentially compromising patient
      outcomes. Furthermore, post-hoc explanation techniques like SHAP (Lundberg
      in A unified approach to interpreting model predictions. arXiv:1705.07874,
      2017) and LIME (Ribeiro et al. in “Why should I trust you?” Explaining the
      predictions of any classifier. In Proceedings of the 22nd ACM SIGKDD
      International Conference on knowledge discovery and data mining, pp
      1135–1144, 2016), while offering insights into complex models, can more
      than double inference times (Abgrall et al. in Crit Care 28:301, 2024),
      significantly impeding workflow efficiency, particularly in time-sensitive
      clinical environments. Adding to these concerns, the integration of XAI
      can elevate operational costs by 10-15%, potentially contributing to
      clinician burnout and hindering widespread adoption (Dhiman et al. in
      Information 14:541, 2023; Hulsen in AI 4:652–666, 2023). While these
      challenges are significant, they must be weighed against potential
      long-term benefits in diagnostic accuracy, workflow efficiency, and
      regulatory compliance, as documented in recent implementation studies
      (Davenport and Kalakota in Future Healthc J 6:94–98, 2019; Sayres et al.
      in Ophthalmology 126:552–564, 2019). The multifaceted challenges highlight
      the inherent tension between transparency, performance, and
      cost-effectiveness in the application of AI within healthcare. This review
      synthesizes the evidence for these trade-offs, drawing upon technical
      findings on performance degradation, clinical data illustrating workflow
      disruptions, and economic analyses quantifying the financial burdens
      associated with XAI. We demonstrate that relying solely on post-hoc
      explanation methods can undermine the predictive power of high-performing
      models by approximately 3.8% (Amann et al. in BMC Med Inform Decis Mak
      20:310, 2020) and add 30–40 s to each patient case. By examining promising
      emerging solutions, including hybrid models that integrate interpretable
      components directly into their architectures (Koh et al. in Concept
      bottleneck models. In: Proceedings of the International Conference on
      machine learning, PMLR, pp. 5338–5348.
      https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/koh20a.html, 2020), and by advocating
      for clinician-centered evaluation frameworks, we aim to identify viable
      pathways to maintain diagnostic accuracy without incurring prohibitive
      costs or unacceptable latency. Ultimately, we argue that aligning AI
      design with the practical demands of clinical workflows, establishing
      standardized metrics for interpretability, and embedding transparency
      directly within model architectures are crucial steps towards realizing
      trustworthy and cost-efficient decision support. Through this pragmatic
      lens, balancing inherent interpretability with computational efficiency,
      and considering both immediate costs and long-term benefits, XAI can
      transition from a theoretical ideal to a sustainable and truly
      transformative asset for patient care. © The Author(s), under exclusive
      licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025.
