DA  - 2026
PB  - Walter de Gruyter GmbH
AB  - Many industries have experienced rapid artificial intelligence (AI) and
      robotics penetration that has produced significant alterations in daily
      life, shifting social structures. These transformations occur through
      mathematical models while delivering capabilities, which include data
      analysis and prediction-making and decision support. These models demand
      intense moral and social evaluation because of the extensive ethical
      implications they create. This chapter analyses AI and robotic
      mathematical modeling outcomes through the examination of three essential
      fields: inclination and decency, straightforwardness and responsibility,
      and open recognition. Scientific models source their inclination from
      multiple sources including recorded information alongside plan preferences
      and social-cultural standards. Models that clone preexisting biases in
      their data propagation systems generate discriminatory outputs throughout
      fundamental operations including recruitment processes and law enforcement
      and financial services. The example analyzed throughout this chapter
      demonstrates how biased assessment techniques cause problems that
      necessitate moral guidelines to lead development and management.
      Straightforwardness and responsibility represent major significant
      concerns alongside the other issues. Users lose their ability to
      understand AI framework decision processes because these systems operate
      as "dark boxes"in the field. The lack of transparency causes moral
      problems specifically in critical domains such as healthcare and criminal
      justice because lifesaving choices depend on it. The author supports the
      creation of logical AI systems, which prioritize understanding clients
      better and enhance ethical decisions. Mathematical models must be openly
      understood to achieve proper implementation success. Social views about AI
      alongside robotic technology impact both the recognition and trust that
      the public develops toward these technological systems. Nonetheless when
      public opinion perceives these developments as unfair or one-sided the
      open population may choose to resist the appropriation of these
      technologies. A detailed assessment appears in the chapter about
      communication success and open engagement strategies for building trust
      and reducing fears related to AI and robotics. The chapter accomplishes a
      complete examination of the moral-social effects that scientific models
      have on AI and robotics by uniting its main points. A trusted honed system
      that embodies moral principles including decency and accountability
      alongside transparency needs public collaboration between ethicists and
      technologists and policymakers to materialize. Full societal value from AI
      and robotics demands immediate action to solve their ethical challenges
      according to the central message of this chapter. © 2026 Walter de Gruyter
      GmbH, Berlin/Boston, Genthiner Straße 13, 10785 Berlin.
