TI  - Ethical Considerations and Transparency in Intelligent Agent Operations
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The widespread introduction of intelligent agents into sectors such as
      healthcare, education, finance, customer service, and public
      administration has raised growing concerns about the ethics of their
      design, the transparency of their operation, and the governance of their
      accountability. These systems can automate complex tasks and provide
      personalized services. Still, their lack of transparency, heavy reliance
      on data, and tendency to perpetuate societal biases pose risks to privacy,
      fairness, autonomy, and trust in institutions. This study examines recent
      work on ethical issues and the transparency of intelligent agents. It
      identifies the black-box dilemma, algorithmic bias, and failures in data
      governance as focal points. In addition, it offers examples from
      recruitment and clinical decision support to illustrate how abstract
      ethical principles become real issues when automated decisions affect
      people's opportunities, rights, and well-being. It suggests that
      trustworthy intelligent agents can be achieved only through a
      socio-technical setup that integrates explainable and interpretable models
      and incorporates fairness evaluation, privacy-by-design, human
      intervention, audit logs, and regulatory compliance. In essence, it
      recommends a combination of technical fixes and juridical, administrative,
      and participatory modes of governance as the way to build intelligent
      agents that not only are a channel of innovation but also, to the greatest
      extent possible, embody and support the values of transparency,
      accountability, human autonomy, and social welfare. © 2026 IEEE.
