TI  - Explainability in AI-Driven Medical Diagnostics
SP  - 3-9
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) and, in particular, deep learning have
      transformed medical image analysis and computer-aided diagnosis.
      State-of-the-art convolutional and transformer-based architectures now
      achieve or surpass human-level performance in tasks such as tumor
      detection, organ segmentation, or disease classification in radiology and
      histopathology. Yet these systems are typically realized as 'black boxes',
      whose internal representations and decision pathways are opaque to
      clinicians. In safety-critical, high-stakes environments such as
      healthcare, this lack of transparency undermines trust, complicates
      regulatory approval, and poses ethical risks related to bias,
      accountability, and patient autonomy. This paper presents a structured
      overview of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) for AI-driven
      medical diagnostics. We first clarify the concepts of interpretability and
      explainability and motivate their importance for clinical decision
      support. We then present a simplified taxonomy of XAI methods along four
      axes: relation to the model architecture, scope of explanation, timing of
      application, and relation to the underlying model. Within this framework,
      we review three major families of approaches that are already being
      explored in medical imaging: visualization-based methods,
      model-distillation and surrogate modeling, and intrinsically interpretable
      models. Throughout the paper, we discuss representative medical
      applications to illustrate how XAI methods can highlight clinically
      meaningful regions or concepts. We conclude by highlighting the role of
      XAI for regulatory compliance, ethical responsibility, and
      human-in-the-loop governance, and briefly outline future directions of
      research in XAI systems. © 2025 IEEE.
