TI  - Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning for Predictive Food Safety
T2  - Food Safety in the Digital Age
SP  - 107-126
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - wiley
AB  - This chapter surveys how artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning
      (ML) are shifting food safety from reactive testing to predictive,
      data-driven control across global supply chains. We outline AI/ML
      (rule-based vs. data-driven systems, ML, and deep learning) and the role
      of big data in training robust models. Applications span pathogen and
      chemical contaminant detection (utilising computer vision, spectroscopy,
      and metagenomics), real-time quality evaluation and foreign-object
      detection, early-warning systems for crops, predictive risk modelling of
      contamination, and time-series shelf-life forecasting. Beyond hazard
      control, AI supports regulatory compliance through NLP-assisted document
      analysis and audit automation, and improves inventory and recall
      management via demand forecasting, perishability optimisation, and rapid
      root-cause analysis—often in concert with IoT sensors and blockchain for
      end-to-end traceability. Evidence from pilots and industry deployments
      shows efficiency gains, waste reduction, and faster, more targeted
      interventions. The chapter also addresses implementation challenges, data
      quality, algorithmic bias and transparency, model governance, and skills
      and cost barriers, and proposes pathways for responsible adoption
      (explainable AI, standards and validation, privacy-preserving data
      sharing, and multi-stakeholder collaboration). We conclude with prospects
      for integrating AI/ML with IoT and digital traceability to develop
      preventive, resilient, and trustworthy food safety systems. © 2027 John
      Wiley & Sons Ltd.
