TI  - Explainable AI and Large Language Model in Mental Health Support System
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - With recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI) and natural language
      processing (NLP), digital mental health support systems have emerged as
      promising tools to improve access to psychological care. Large language
      models (LLMs) have significantly enhanced conversational quality,
      contextual reasoning, and personalization in AI-assisted mental health
      counseling systems (MHCS). However, the black-box nature of LLMs raises
      critical concerns related to explainability, ethical accountability, user
      trust, and clinical reliability, particularly in sensitive mental
      healthcare applications. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis and
      evaluation of explainable AI (XAI) integrated LLM-based mental health
      support systems. A modular architecture is proposed that explicitly
      incorporates emotion analysis, explainability mechanisms, ethical
      guardrails, and human-in-the-loop oversight into the LLM reasoning
      pipeline. The system is evaluated against traditional machine
      learning-based and pure LLM-based approaches using multiple criteria,
      including response quality, explainability, ethical safety, and escalation
      accuracy. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed
      XAI-integrated MHCS significantly improves interpretability and user trust
      while reducing harmful and biased responses, without compromising
      conversational effectiveness. The study further discusses ethical, legal,
      and social implications associated with deploying LLMs in mental
      healthcare and identifies key challenges related to transparency, bias,
      and responsible deployment. By emphasizing explainability and ethical
      alignment as core design principles, this work contributes toward the
      development of trustworthy, clinically viable, and responsible AI-driven
      mental health support systems. © 2026 IEEE.
