AB  - Artificial intelligence has a growing presence in healthcare, which helps
      in diagnosing, predicting risks, clinical decision- making, and planning
      of resources. Although they are performing well, most AI systems are hard
      to understand, posing issues of trust, accountability, and safe clinical
      application. Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) fills this gap and
      allows understanding how models produce their results, as well as enabling
      informed human supervision. The chapter covers the history of AI in
      healthcare, explains the major concepts of XAI including interpretability,
      transparency, and global versus local explanation, and provides an
      effective taxonomy of explainability methods. Its use in medical imaging,
      clinical decision support, disease risk prediction, precision medicine,
      and health economics are mentioned. Such ethical, regulatory, and
      implementation issues are discussed, as well as new directions such as
      explainability- by- design and human- in- the- loop systems. Combined,
      these views make XAI a vital part of reliable and accountable healthcare
      AI. © 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.
