TI  - A Review of Deep Learning Models and Explainable Artificial Intelligence
      for Pathology Recognition
SP  - 313-322
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The accelerated development of artificial intelligence (AI), particularly
      deep learning (DL), has established datadriven models as essential tools
      in medical imaging analysis and clinical decision support. Nevertheless,
      the complex, non-linear architectures of many DL models regularly yield
      predictions that are opaque and difficult for clinicians to interpret, a
      phenomenon known as the black-box problem. To address this, the research
      community has developed explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) methods
      to provide clearer, more intuitive explanations of AI decisions, thereby
      promoting trust in AI-based healthcare solutions. This review examines the
      roles of DL models and XAI in pathology recognition, with a focus on
      medical imaging. A structured search was conducted over major scientific
      databases, including PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science. Additionally,
      ResearchRabbit and Perplexity AI were used as citation-based complementary
      tools, covering literature from November 2019 to December 2025. 181
      studies were eligible and included in this analysis. Findings suggest that
      XAI techniques such as Grad-CAM, Grad-CAM++, SHAP, LIME, DeepLIFT, and
      counterfactual explanations are commonly employed to interpret model
      predictions, particularly in ensemble and convolutional architectures,
      including Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Transformers,
      MM-Transformer, Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), and Vision-Language models
      (VLMs) based on CLIP. The integration of XAI into medical AI systems
      enhances not only interpretability but also accountability, fairness, and
      clinician trust, which are critical for adoption in sensitive clinical
      domains. © 2026 IEEE.
