TI  - Advantages, drawbacks, emerging research challenges, and future scope in
      artificial intelligence-driven healthcare diagnostics and optimization
T2  - Data-Driven Diagnostics and Disease Prediction with AI Optimization
SP  - 339-344
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Elsevier
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming healthcare and becoming an
      enabler for optimization of diagnostics, treatment planning, as well as
      operational management. In this chapter, we explore the coming
      intersection of data—and diagnostic—with AI, an intersection through which
      we believe AI can transform the delivery of healthcare. According to the
      same algorithms, AI enables greater proactive and precise operation of the
      healthcare system, allowing them to detect the disease at its early
      stages, create a personalized treatment plan, and improve the patient’s
      life. By incorporating wearable technologies and Internet of Things
      devices, continuous patient monitoring is also made possible and is put
      into real-time interventions and is also to lessen the hospital
      dependency. In this chapter, we examine how AI is advancing drug discovery
      and clinical trials, faster analysis of huge datasets enabling drugs to be
      developed much more quickly, and with greater chances of success. The
      potential use of federated learning models as a potential solution to
      handle the data privacy challenges while allowing collaborative work
      between institutions, while abiding by privacy regulations is highlighted.
      Holistic and precise diagnostics are being paved by advanced AI-driven
      decision support systems that can interpret complex multimodal data.
      Operational efficiencies are another space AI helps, optimizing resource
      allocation, predicting patient inflow, and automating several routine
      administrative tasks. It explores what integrating quantum computing into
      AI would mean and how that could be used to answer difficult healthcare
      problems like protein folding and complex data analysis. Moreover, ethical
      concerns and the need for explainable AI systems are identified, to
      increase confidence in the trustworthiness and reliability in clinical
      settings. In this chapter, we offer an in-depth look at the progressive
      roll out of AI in healthcare, from its status today and into the future
      for a resilient, intelligent, and equitable healthcare ecosystem. © 2026
      Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
