PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Repetitive behavioral disorders (RBDs), particularly autism spectrum
      disorder (ASD) and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD),
      require accurate and timely diagnostic methods because conventional
      assessments are largely subjective and behavior-based. Recent advances in
      artificial intelligence (AI), wearable sensing, Internet of Things (IoT),
      and digital twin (DT) technologies have enabled intelligent data-driven
      diagnostic frameworks. This review presents a comprehensive survey of
      AI-Assisted RBD diagnosis from 2016 to 2025 using biomarkers (BMs) such as
      electroencephalography (EEG), gait, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and
      kinesics. The review covers major stages of the diagnostic pipeline,
      including data acquisition, preprocessing, feature extraction,
      classification, multimodal fusion, and DT-Assisted healthcare
      architectures. Public benchmark datasets, machine learning (ML) and deep
      learning (DL) methods, and multimodal diagnostic strategies are
      comparatively analyzed. The findings indicate that DL and multimodal
      frameworks generally achieve superior performance by integrating
      complementary neurological, physiological, and behavioral information.
      Emerging technologies such as explainable AI (XAI), federated learning
      (FL), edge intelligence, and DTs also show strong potential for
      personalized and privacy-preserving healthcare. This study further
      discusses major challenges that limit clinical deployment, including
      limited standardized datasets, small sample sizes, poor model
      generalization, interoperability issues, cybersecurity risks, and
      computational complexity. Future research should therefore emphasize
      large-scale multimodal datasets, explainable and trustworthy AI,
      privacy-preserving collaborative learning, and DT-enabled clinical
      decision-support systems for reliable and scalable RBD diagnosis. © 2014
      IEEE.
