TI  - Security and Privacy Issues in Data Processing with Predictive Models
T2  - Predictive Methods in Next-Generation Computing: An Approach Toward
      Sustainability
SP  - 193-206
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - wiley
AB  - With the advent of big data and machine learning, the increased capacity
      to process personal information with predictive models threatens issues
      regarding privacy and security more so now than ever before. Now, replace
      every noun in those sentences with any number of other sectors that are
      likely to become thrashed by unprecedented versions of predictive
      algorithms, loaded up on record levels of private information. However,
      the availability of these systems raises obvious questions of bias in an
      algorithmic configuration, unauthorized access prevention, and data
      security. Data security is a significant issue with data processing using
      predictive models. This involves ensuring that the security of
      confidential data and sensitive information is protected so it does not
      become vulnerable to cyber attacks, e-gaming hacking, or password
      cracking. Implementing strong encryption methods, access control systems,
      and secure storage protocols can help reduce the severity of these
      security threats. Also, the use of Personally Identifiable Information
      (PII) introduces major privacy issues in predictive modeling. Privacy is a
      right that belongs to the individual, and there are strong data protection
      laws such as GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation) or CCPA (California
      Consumer Privacy Act), which dictate how predictive models should be
      personally identifiable. By using privacy-preserving algorithms,
      anonymization approaches, and differential privacy strategies, we can
      protect the privacy of people while still gaining valuable insights from
      data. Even more seriously, the issue of bias and prejudice in our
      prediction models risks worsening existing societal injustices and gaps.
      Biased training sets, algorithm design decisions, or even societal
      inclinations can be blamed for data bias. To ensure that predictive
      modeling is fair, it is important to take a careful look at the data
      collection (recording), model creation (generating), and evaluation
      measures. More open and accountable prediction algorithms are, in part, a
      response to the general use of ever-more pervasive predictive analytics.
      When a prediction model is live, everyone involved needs an understanding
      of what goes into the models to make decisions and get out. Model
      interpretability tools, explainable AI methodology, and auditability
      metrics can likely help improve intrusion detection system for
      unstructured data (IDSUD) transparency in a way that allows stakeholders
      to evaluate the accuracy of both prediction models (i.e., whether they
      leverage single sources) as well as their equity over protected variables.
      In summary, predictive models offer the potential for improved
      decision-making and innovation but also raise privacy and security
      challenges. These two big problems have to be part-solved via robust
      security measures, better privacyenhancing technology mitigating bias, and
      transparency programs. While enterprises can benefit from big data
      analytics, the processing of the data with predictive models must be kept
      secure and private to protect individual rights and societal values. ©
      2025 Scrivener Publishing LLC. All rights reserved.
