TI  - Integrating large language models (LLMs) in climate-smart water
      management: Future approaches and innovations
T2  - Advances in Chemical Pollution, Environmental Management and Protection
VL  - 15
SP  - 149-189
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Elsevier B.V.
AB  - Climate change, urbanization, and population growth pose significant
      challenges to water management, necessitating more adaptive and
      sustainable approaches. This article explores the possibilities of
      integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) into climate-smart water
      management to improve decision-making, predictive modeling, data analysis,
      and stakeholder engagement. Conventional water management systems rely
      heavily on historical data and centralized decision-making and are
      generally becoming increasingly inadequate to address the complexities of
      climate variability, including extreme weather events. Emerging
      technologies, including Artificial Intelligence (AI), Machine Learning
      (ML), Remote Sensing (RS), Geographic Information Systems (GIS), and the
      Internet of Things (IoT), are already transforming conventional water
      management, but LLMs open up new capabilities. Their advanced Natural
      Language Processing (NLP) and multimodal data comprehension empower the
      synthesis of vast, heterogeneous datasets, uncovering understandings that
      traditional tools might be missing. Moreover, this article highlights key
      applications of LLMs across four areas: data analysis and interpretation,
      decision support systems, predictive modeling and forecasting, and
      enriching communication for stakeholder engagement. Case studies
      demonstrate how LLMs improve forecasting accuracy, support more informed
      decision-making, and advance inclusive, transparent engagement among
      diverse stakeholders. Challenges, such as technological limitations, data
      privacy concerns, and biases in model outputs, are addressed alongside
      ethical considerations in deploying LLMs for critical water management
      functions. Furthermore, the study examines future trends, including
      developing more efficient models, improved integration with other
      technologies, and the increasing role of explainable AI. It concludes by
      emphasizing the revolutionary potential of LLMs in making water management
      more resilient, equitable, and sustainable in the face of climate change.
