TI  - A Prototype Artificial Intelligence Model for Tax Fraud Detection: An
      Applied Study
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Tax fraud represents a persistent challenge for revenue authorities
      worldwide, leading to significant fiscal losses and undermining public
      trust in taxation systems. Conventional enforcement approaches, largely
      based on manual audits and rule-driven compliance checks, are increasingly
      ineffective against sophisticated fraud schemes emerging in digital
      economies. Recent advances in Artificial Intelligence (AI) offer promising
      opportunities to transform tax administration through intelligent
      automation, predictive analytics, and enhanced risk assessment. This paper
      proposes a prototype Artificial Intelligence framework for tax fraud
      detection from an applied conceptual perspective. Rather than developing a
      fully operational machine learning system, the study presents a structured
      model illustrating how AI techniques can be systematically embedded into
      tax governance processes. The framework integrates taxpayer financial
      data, preprocessing modules, feature engineering mechanisms, and AI-driven
      risk profiling to support audit prioritization and compliance monitoring.
      The study explores practical applications of AI in identifying abnormal
      income patterns, irregular filing behavior, suspicious tax credit claims,
      and shell entities. Broader implications for forensic accounting,
      compliance management, and revenue administration are discussed. The paper
      also highlights critical challenges related to AI implementation,
      including data quality constraints, privacy and security concerns,
      algorithmic transparency, and ethical considerations. The study concludes
      that Artificial Intelligence can substantially strengthen proactive tax
      fraud detection when complemented by institutional oversight and
      professional judgment. The proposed prototype framework offers a
      foundational reference for policymakers, tax authorities, and researchers
      aiming to leverage intelligent technologies in modern revenue systems. ©
      2026 IEEE.
