TI  - Towards Computing Futures in Education: An Adaptive Decision Model for
      Teacher-School Matching with Explainable Outputs
VL  - 2155 LNNS
SP  - 298-310
PY  - 2027
DA  - 2027
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - Teacher-school matching in many systems remains manual, slow, and hard to
      justify, leading to subject mismatch and uneven distribution. This paper
      proposes an adaptive decision model that connects policy rules with
      algorithmic optimization to support transparent placement decisions. The
      work follows a Design Science Research (DSR) approach and draws on
      decision theory and multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM). Criteria and
      weights are elicited using Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP), alternatives
      are scored with Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal
      Solution (TOPSIS), and feasible assignments are searched using a
      soft-computing genetic algorithm (GA) under real policy constraints
      (quotas, subject requirements, distance, hardship). We position the model
      against matching-market ideas from the assignment literature and argue
      that explainable outputs showing the top factors behind each
      recommendation are necessary to build trust with stakeholders and to
      support appeals or audits. A prototype support system operationalizes the
      model and is evaluated on simulated scenarios and state cases. Results
      indicate faster processing, lower teacher–subject mismatch, and strong
      agreement with expert judgements, with positive usability feedback from
      practitioners. The paper contributes (i) a clear framework linking
      criteria, constraints, and outcomes, (ii) a prescriptive, explainable
      model for public-sector placement, and (iii) evidence from an
      implementable prototype. The approach is practical, auditable, and
      adaptable to future policy updates.Across simulated and state cases,
      processing time decreased by [x]%, subject mismatch fell by [y] pp., and
      agreement with experts remained substantial (κ = [z]). © The Author(s),
      under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
