TI  - “There is a Place for it”—Practitioners’ Perspectives on Situated Ethical
      Acceptability of AI Technologies for Mental Health Practice: Qualitative
      Study
T2  - JMIR Ment. Health
JF  - JMIR Mental Health
VL  - 13
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - JMIR Publications Inc.
AB  - Background: Design research on AI technologies for mental health has
      expanded rapidly, yet existing work has largely focused on practitioners’
      perspectives within isolated therapeutic stages or specific services. As a
      result, there is limited understanding of how practitioners view the role
      of AI across the full therapeutic journey, including assessment,
      diagnosis, and treatment, or how these perspectives can be translated into
      actionable and ethically grounded design guidance. This lack of
      stage-specific guidance introduces several risks: (1) the development of
      AI tools that practitioners may not trust or adopt, (2) heightened ethical
      concerns, particularly in diagnosis where bias and erosion of human
      judgment are most likely, and (3) missed opportunities to leverage AI to
      support ongoing assessment and treatment adaptation. Objective: This study
      aimed to explore mental health practitioners’ perspectives on how AI may
      support or hinder therapeutic practice across all 3 therapeutic stages,
      with particular attention to their ethical considerations. Methods: We
      conducted semistructured interviews with 18 mental health practitioners to
      explore how AI may support or hinder therapeutic practice across
      assessment, diagnosis, and treatment. Data were analyzed thematically, and
      ethical challenges were organized using 4 biomedical principles. This
      study received approval from the Lancaster University Institutional Ethics
      Committee (ID 3530). Results: Findings suggest a stage-specific pattern:
      practitioners identified valuable roles for AI in supporting clinical
      assessment and treatment, while expressing strong resistance to autonomous
      AI diagnosis, which was regarded as the most ethically sensitive stage.
      Across contexts, AI was seen as supporting and validating, rather than
      replacing, clinical judgment. Ethical concerns were organized using these
      principles, with key issues including data validity, inclusivity, privacy,
      transparency, and explainability. Grounded in these findings, four design
      directions are proposed: (1) AI-enabled clinical assessment hubs for
      analyzing multimodal patient data to support transparent decision-making;
      (2) natural language processing-based tools to enable richer understanding
      of patients’ lived experiences; (3) new models of trust to support
      ethically sensitive AI-assisted diagnostic decision-making; and (4) large
      language model–powered personalized and adaptive mental health support
      systems that maintain human-AI partnership and preserve practitioners’
      central role in care. Conclusions: This study provides a stage-specific
      understanding of practitioners’ perspectives on AI use across assessment,
      diagnosis, and treatment and systematically frames ethical challenges
      using biomedical principles. The findings translate practitioner insights
      into actionable design guidance for ethically informed AI-supported mental
      health practice. ©Abeer Alotaibi, Corina Sas.
