TI  - Explainable AI for Smart Agriculture: Interpretable Agronomic Prediction
      and Decision Support in Rwanda
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Smart agriculture increasingly relies on artificial intelligence (AI) to
      support yield prediction, nutrient management, climate-aware planning, and
      data-driven farm decision-making. However, many high-performing
      agricultural prediction models remain insufficiently transparent for
      practical adoption, particularly farmer-dominated settings where users
      must understand and trust model recommendations before acting on them.
      This paper presents an explainable AI framework for smart agriculture in
      Rwanda, focusing on interpretable agronomic prediction using integrated
      soil, weather, and crop-yield data. Using data from the Rwanda Soil
      Information System (RwaSIS), official seasonal agricultural statistics,
      and Meteo Rwanda climate information, we developed and evaluated several
      machine learning models, with LightGBM achieving the strongest predictive
      performance (R2 = 0.691). To improve transparency and usability, we
      incorporated SHAP and LIME as complementary post hoc explanation
      techniques and compared them using interpretability, computational
      efficiency, fidelity, and stakeholder usability criteria. The results show
      that SHAP provides stronger global interpretability and greater
      explanation fidelity, while LIME offers intuitive local explanations that
      are easier for nontechnical users to understand. The findings position
      explainability as a core requirement for trustworthy AI-driven
      agricultural decision support. We argue that explainable smart-agriculture
      systems can better support farmers, extension services, and policymakers
      by improving transparency, accountability, and actionability in agronomic
      decision-making. ©2026 IEEE.
