AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly embedded in consumer facing
      decision environments, where it supports or automates recommendations,
      credit assessment, personalised pricing, risk classification, digital
      contracting, and information filtering. This article examines AI driven
      consumer decision making from an integrated decision support, legal, and
      governance perspective. It analyses how opacity, autonomy, data
      dependence, algorithmic bias, manipulation, and limited explainability can
      affect decision quality, consumer autonomy, fairness, and access to
      redress. Drawing on national and international regulatory frameworks and
      practical cases involving ecommerce, facial recognition, algorithmic
      pricing, financial services, and automated advice, the study evaluates how
      technical and institutional controls can reduce decision risks. Attention
      is given to transparency, human oversight, data governance,
      accountability, liability allocation, and the ability of consumers to
      challenge consequential automated decisions. The article conceptualises
      regulation as a decision control mechanism that complements technical
      safeguards in AI enabled decision support systems. It further proposes a
      responsible AI decision governance perspective in which predictive
      performance is assessed alongside fairness, explainability, robustness,
      consumer autonomy, and accountability. The resulting framework connects
      computer based decision processes with economic decision making,
      organisational governance, and consumer protection, thereby supporting the
      design and deployment of more trustworthy AI assisted decision systems. ©
      2026, Awrak Thakafieh. All rights reserved.
