TI  - Interpretable Machine Learning for Cost Estimation in Public Restoration
      Projects: An Empirical Analysis of Turkish Cultural Heritage Data
T2  - Adv. Civ. Eng.
JF  - Advances in Civil Engineering
VL  - 2026
IS  - 1
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - John Wiley and Sons Ltd
AB  - Globally, the primary barrier to adopting advanced analytics in public
      administration is often not a lack of data but the legal incompatibility
      of “black-box” predictive models with statutory requirements for
      transparency. This study addresses this governance challenge by developing
      an interpretable machine learning (ML) framework designed as an auditable
      solution for predicting pretender costs under strict regulatory
      environments, such as Turkish Public Procurement Laws No. 4734 and 5018.
      Utilizing a unique dataset of 578 projects from Türkiye’s Directorate
      General of Cultural Assets and Museums, this study suggests that
      inherently interpretable models, specifically classification and
      regression trees (CART) and chi-square automatic interaction detection
      (CHAID), outperform traditional linear regression. Validated through
      10-fold cross-validation and a temporal holdout protocol, the CART model
      yielded a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) on a logarithmic scale of
      8.200%, a root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.250, and a coefficient of
      determination (R2) of 0.663, identifying critical nonlinear risk
      thresholds, such as a four-fold cost escalation beyond a 100-day project
      duration. A key outcome was a preliminarily validated prototype
      decision-support tool that generated a “Decision Justification Report,”
      explicitly mapping project risks to auditable thresholds. This study
      provides a preliminarily validated, scalable framework for deploying AI in
      regulated public finance domains, with potential applicability to other
      similar regulatory contexts, helping to reconcile the tension between
      predictive power and institutional accountability. Copyright © 2026 Ali
      Haydar Akçakale and Mehmet Nurettin Uğural. Advances in Civil Engineering
      published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
