TI  - Differentially Private and Explainable Federated Learning for ICU
      Mortality Prediction
SP  - 892-898
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - Accurate ICU mortality prediction is vital for timely care but is
      constrained by strict privacy rules and the need for interpretable models.
      This paper proposes DP-IG-FL, a federated learning framework that ensures
      data privacy while enabling transparent and collaborative prediction
      across multiple hospitals. The framework is built on an LSTM-based
      federated architecture, where Differentially Private Stochastic Gradient
      Descent (DP-SGD) is employed to prevent information leakage during model
      updates, and Integrated Gradients (IG) is used to provide feature-level
      explanations aligned with clinical reasoning. The proposed approach is
      evaluated using two widely adopted ICU benchmarks, MIMIC-III and eICU,
      under realistic multi-hospital settings. Experimental results show that
      DP-IG-FL achieves an AUROC of 0.89, outperforming standard FedAvg-LSTM by
      5.3% and centralized LSTM models by 2.4%. Notably, the model retains more
      than 97% of its predictive performance even under a strict privacy budget
      of ϵ = 1.0, demonstrating strong resilience to privacy constraints.
      Explainability highlights key predictors like creatinine, heart rate,
      blood pressure, and oxygen levels, improving trust in the model. Overall,
      DP-IG-FL illustrates that high predictive accuracy, rigorous privacy
      guarantees, and clinically interpretable insights can be achieved
      simultaneously, supporting the development of trustworthy AI systems for
      critical-care decision support. © 2026 IEEE.
