TI  - Designing Interpretable and Energy Efficient AI Pipelines for
      Sustainability Analytics: A Proof-of-Concept Framework
VL  - 2119 LNNS
SP  - 327-338
PY  - 2027
DA  - 2027
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - Environmental, Social, and Governance analytics increasingly adopt
      artificial intelligence, yet many approaches emphasize predictive accuracy
      while overlooking interpretability, governance alignment, and
      computational sustainability. This study proposes a design-oriented,
      proof-of-concept framework that repositions machine learning as an
      interpretive and governance-aware analytical tool rather than a predictive
      model. Based on ESG standards from GRI, SASB, and TCFD, the framework
      integrates explainable artificial intelligence and energy-efficient
      computing principles, including green algorithm considerations. Through a
      lightweight machine learning configuration, the study illustrates how
      sustainability data can support interpretive analysis, pattern
      exploration, and variable sensitivity assessment without making empirical
      performance claims. This research contributes to responsible and
      sustainable ICT-enabled analytics by offering a transparent and replicable
      reference for ESG-oriented system design. © The Author(s), under exclusive
      license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
