AB  - Fleet operations require maintenance planning under resource constraints
      to sustain operational readiness across sequential missions. Existing
      selective maintenance research addresses primarily single-system scenarios
      and relies on static optimization, overlooking the operational
      interdependence between mission assignment and component maintenance in
      fleet-level multimission contexts, where the combinatorial state-action
      space renders conventional methods intractable. This article proposes a
      selective maintenance framework based on hierarchical deep reinforcement
      learning for fleet-level multimission operations. First, a fleet-level
      selective maintenance strategy with two hierarchical decision levels is
      formulated, jointly optimizing mission assignment and component
      maintenance based on real-time equipment health. Second, the problem is
      formulated as a hierarchical Markov decision process that decomposes the
      joint optimization into coordinated upper-level mission assignment and
      lower-level maintenance selection. Third, a hierarchical deep
      reinforcement learning algorithm is developed with a parameter-sharing
      mechanism across homogeneous equipment, reducing action space complexity
      and improving sample efficiency. Numerical experiments demonstrate that
      the proposed method achieves higher mission reliability and cumulative
      reward than static and flat reinforcement learning baselines. Sensitivity
      analysis shows that optimal fleet size depends on the adopted maintenance
      strategy, providing a basis for fleet sizing decisions that balance
      operational redundancy and fixed costs.
