TI  - A Cognitive Load Theory-Based Study on the Impact Mechanisms of Generative
      AI Application Patterns on Learning Engagement: Metacognitive Regulation
      and Disciplinary Differences from a Science and Engineering Perspective
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Smart City and
      Sustainable Development, SCSD 2026
SP  - 240-248
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery, Inc
AB  - Based on Cognitive Load Theory (CLT) and Contingency Theory, this study
      constructs a theoretical model examining how Generative Artificial
      Intelligence (GenAI) application patterns influence learning engagement,
      with particular emphasis on the moderating effects of metacognitive
      ability and disciplinary type (science/engineering vs. humanities). Data
      were collected from 727 undergraduate students in industry-oriented
      universities (effective response rate: 88.2%), with 52.2% in technical
      majors, 40.4% in applied engineering, and 7.4% in humanities. Structural
      Equation Modeling (SEM) and hierarchical regression analysis were employed
      for hypothesis testing. Key technical findings: (1) Metacognition-guided
      usage (active inquiry, critical integration) demonstrated significantly
      higher cognitive engagement than heuristic-dependent usage (3.85 vs. 3.15,
      t=9.637, p technical (0.562) > humanities (0.851, n=31). Technical
      contributions: This study provides algorithmic governance evidence for
      GenAI deployment in higher education informatization, proposing a human-AI
      collaborative learning design framework with practical value for
      Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) development. This aligns with ITS
      effectiveness research, which establishes that adaptive systems with
      mixed-initiative dialogue capabilities yield comparable learning gains to
      human tutoring when properly calibrated to learner characteristics.
      Emerging AI education frameworks further corroborate our findings
      regarding the contingent role of disciplinary contexts in optimizing
      human-AI collaboration, while educational cobots and smart classroom
      implementations extend the practical applicability of our proposed usage
      pattern taxonomy. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
