TI  - Examining Intelligent Tutoring Systems and Their AI Underpinnings in the
      Design of the Future of Learning
T2  - ICNAS 2025 - 7th International Conference on Networking and Advanced
      Systems
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - This Intelligent tutoring systems (ITS), which generate scalable,
      adaptive, and individualized learning, have recently become one of the
      most promising areas of artificial intelligence in learning. This survey
      identifies promising ITS architectures, such as AutoTutor, DeepTutor,
      ELM-ART, Wayang Outpost, and DeMINT and discusses their underlying
      technologies, pedagogies, and learning impacts. We propose the need for
      ongoing scrutiny to recognize challenges in the field, including content
      authoring costs, occurring scalability across disciplines and languages,
      privacy and security of students, and potential bias and learners'
      autonomy. We suggest various aspects of development to improve the
      challenges and limitations of ITS, including: The consideration of
      pedagogical and ethical issues in the adoption of large language models
      (LLMs), the exclusion of cultural factors in development and use, the need
      for explainable AI, and design which excludes and is inclusive to
      neurodiverse and marginalized learners. This work has acknowledged the
      nature of interdisciplinary context, with this work suggesting a clear
      need for collaboration to ensure that future ITS are pedagogically sound,
      technologically enhanced, ethical, and caters for diverse learners. © 2025
      IEEE.
