TI  - Work-in-Progress: LOGIA - A Proposal for a Learning Framework Supported by
      Generative AI for Logical Reasoning in Programming
T2  - IEEE Global Engineering Education Conference, EDUCON
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - IEEE Computer Society
AB  - Programming skills, such as logical reasoning, abstraction, and
      computational thinking, continue to pose challenges in introductory
      programming courses due to manual diagnostics, static content, and delayed
      feedback. To address this problem, this work-in-progress presents LOGIA,
      an adaptive framework aimed at modeling and supporting the progression of
      logical reasoning in beginning programming students through cognitive
      diagnosis, adaptation, and data-driven feedback.LOGIA integrates three
      components: (i) an initial diagnosis using the psychometrically validated
      Computational Thinking Test (CTt); (ii) adaptive generation of code
      tracing exercises using language models (LLMs), adjusted to the student's
      cognitive level; and (iii) immediate feedback based on learning analytics
      and knowledge tracing to guide learning trajectories. At this stage, LOGIA
      is defined as a design-level prototype, conceived for future validation in
      real teaching contexts. This approach seeks to overcome the limitations of
      traditional intelligent tutoring systems and isolated generative AI-based
      solutions by integrating diagnosis, adaptation, and feedback within a
      single pedagogical framework. © 2026 IEEE.
