AU  - Zeynalov, I
TI  - How Many Students, or How Many Classrooms? Design Effects for Item
      Analytics in Clustered Learning Platforms
T2  - Measurement
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Routledge
AB  - Learning platforms calibrate item difficulty and discrimination directly
      from response logs and published minimum-sample rules tell developers how
      many responses an item needs. Those rules assume independent responses,
      yet platform responses arrive in intact classrooms. Across two
      structurally different platforms–a diagnostic-question service (15.9
      million responses, 27,613 mathematics items, 11,838 classes) and an
      intelligent tutoring system–I measure the intraclass correlation of item
      correctness within classrooms and its design effect. The effect is
      estimand-specific: for item difficulty the design effect is about 2.9–4.1
      (median classroom intraclass correlation 0.13 and 0.25), so the effective
      sample is a third or less of the nominal count, but for item
      discrimination it is only about 1.4. Crucially, a nominal 95% confidence
      interval for difficulty covers the truth only about 71% of the time and
      drifts slightly lower, not higher, as responses accumulate, while the
      textbook design-effect formula itself overstates the variance by roughly a
      fifth. An i.i.d. baseline covers near 95%, so the shortfall is the
      clustering, not the interval form. I supply corrected sample-size
      planners. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
