AU  - Yildirim, B
TI  - Architecting for autonomy: a federated and explainable AI blueprint for UK
      public services
VL  - 2025
SP  - 13-31
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
AB  - This paper addresses the growing strategic dependency of national Digital
      Public Infrastructure (DPI) on non-jurisdictional hyperscale cloud
      providers, a critical issue for data sovereignty under regulations like
      the UK GDPR. We argue that for high-stakes public services, a new model of
      sovereign, trustworthy AI is required not only for security, but to unlock
      sustainable gains in public sector efficiency. We propose a novel,
      two-tiered federated architecture for developing and deploying
      specialised, explainable Large Language Model (LLM) agents within a
      nation's own sovereign infrastructure. To validate this architecture, we
      present a functional Proof-of-Concept (PoC): a “Bristol Home Energy
      Upgrade Grant (HEUG) Assistant”. The PoC demonstrates complete operational
      independence and provides a practical roadmap for addressing performance,
      sustainability, and procurement at scale. Crucially, we introduce
      procurement-based sovereignty levers, specific tender requirements that
      create market incentives for sovereign solutions, as a novel policy
      mechanism for achieving technological independence. Our work demonstrates
      that it is technically and economically feasible to build performant,
      secure, and trustworthy AI services that enhance citizen engagement while
      upholding national data sovereignty and driving long-term public value.
