TI  - ORCHID-RANKER: An Agentic Adaptive Recommender for Education with Privacy
      Guarantees
SP  - 116-117
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
AB  - Digital learning platforms often recommend what activity a student should
      do next. They usually base this on a few simple signals, such as whether
      the student accepted the activity, answered correctly, and how long it
      took. These systems are often complex and hard for teachers to understand
      or control, and they also need to protect student privacy. We present
      ORCHID-RANKER, a simple decision module that can be added to existing
      recommenders in such platforms without changing them. It keeps track of
      three things for each learner: how ready they seem (knowledge), how
      willing they seem (engagement), and how uncertain the system is. Using
      this information, it adjusts a few simple "dials": how difficult the next
      activities should be, how much to explore new content, how varied the list
      of activities is, and how many activities to show. The goal is to keep
      learners in a "just right"zone where tasks are not too easy and not too
      hard, and to keep them engaged. Behind this decision module, the
      underlying models are trained with strong privacy protection (differential
      privacy), so that individual students cannot be easily identified from the
      data. In simulations on two real course datasets (OULAD and EdNet),
      ORCHID-RANKER led to higher learning and engagement than standard
      recommendation methods, while still maintaining formal privacy guarantees.
      © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
