AB  - Artificial intelligence is increasingly becoming an integral part of
      modern educational technologies, which enable adaptive learning,
      intelligent tutoring, automatic assessment, and personalized content
      delivery. Recent development in LLMs and RAG has improved the quality of
      syllabus-aligned content generation and domain-specific tutoring
      significantly by reducing hallucinations with external knowledge
      grounding. This paper performs a focused review and comparative study of
      some representative AI-driven educational systems, which include MCQ
      generation frameworks, conversational tutors, adaptive learning platforms,
      and generative summarization models. The analysis shows that LLM-based
      tutors have achieved high response accuracy, while hybrid generative
      models demonstrate strong summarization performance; however, persistent
      challenges remain in the areas of factual verification, privacy
      preservation, interpretability of adaptive decisions, and scalability for
      institutional deployment. Specifically, the lack of standardized
      pedagogical benchmarks and the limited support for multimodal learning
      content pose key limitations to the existing long-term potential. This
      paper proposes a compact EduAI framework that integrates RAG-grounded
      generation with lightweight learning analytics and explainable adaptation
      mechanisms. As such, the proposed architecture will be able to provide
      reliable, transparent, and syllabus-aware educational assistance while
      ensuring computational feasibility for real-world academic environments. ©
      The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG
      2026.
