TI  - AI and Computational Intelligence in Education: A Bibliometric Analysis
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The rapid expansion of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Computational
      Intelligence (CI) in educational research has reshaped how teaching,
      learning, and assessment are conceptualised. Despite this growth, existing
      studies have primarily focused on the application of AI in education. At
      the same time, scant attention has been given to the CI dimension,
      resulting in fragmented insights into how both paradigms jointly influence
      educational innovation. This study, therefore, presents a comprehensive
      bibliometric analysis of global scholarly production on AI and CI in
      education, identifying temporal trends, leading authors, institutions and
      countries, key journals, thematic foci, and collaborative networks. Data
      was extracted from the Scopus database, covering the period from 2012 to
      2024. Using bibliometric software (e.g., VOSviewer, Bibliometrix), we
      analysed publications. Results indicate exponential growth in AI and CI
      research in education since 2015, with dominant contributions from China,
      the United States, and Australia, and high-impact journals such as
      Computers & Education, IEEE Transactions on Learning Technologies, and the
      International Journal of AI in Education. Thematic clusters coalesce
      around intelligent tutoring systems, machine learning in adaptive
      learning, deep learning, learning analytics, and educational data mining.
      However, the analysis reveals persistent research gaps, particularly the
      limited exploration of CI applications in resource-constrained educational
      contexts and ethical considerations surrounding AI integration. This
      bibliometric mapping offers scholars, practitioners, and policymakers a
      comprehensive overview of the field, suggesting future research and
      capacitybuilding directions. © 2026 IEEE.
