TI  - Data-driven public administration: contributions of Artificial
      Intelligence to efficient decision-making in public management
T2  - APOSTA-REVISTA DE CIENCIAS SOCIALES
VL  - 115
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
AB  - The objective of this study was to analyze the contributions of Artificial
      Intelligence to the efficiency of decision-making in public management,
      from the perspective of Data-Driven Public Administration. To this end, an
      integrative literature review was conducted using a qualitative approach,
      drawing on the SciELO, Scopus, Directory of Open Access Journals (DOAJ),
      and Google Scholar databases. The search for studies was conducted using
      keywords related to the topic, combined with the Boolean operators AND and
      OR. Inclusion criteria included scientific articles published between 2020
      and 2024, written in Portuguese, developed in the Brazilian context, and
      available free of charge in full text. The results showed that Artificial
      Intelligence makes significant contributions to the modernization of
      public administration, particularly by enabling the processing of large
      volumes of data, supporting predictive analytics, optimizing
      administrative processes, and strengthening evidence-based
      decision-making. It was also found that the use of intelligent systems can
      increase operational efficiency, improve government planning, and enhance
      the delivery of public services. However, the studies highlighted
      challenges related to data governance, algorithmic transparency,
      information security, training for public servants, and the need to align
      technology with the ethical and constitutional principles of public
      administration. It is concluded that Artificial Intelligence constitutes a
      strategic tool for the development of a more efficient, innovative, and
      data-driven public management, provided that its implementation occurs in
      a responsible and transparent manner and is integrated with the State's
      institutional capacities.
