TI  - Mapping the vertical city: AI-driven analysis of volumetric urban space
      use
T2  - Sustain. Cities Soc.
JF  - Sustainable Cities and Society
VL  - 131
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
AB  - High-density mixed use of the built environment and volumetric urban
      landscapes have been used as key planning strategies to address the
      challenges of limited land, exploded urban population, the need for daily
      efficiency, and sustainable development. While many studies have
      investigated the impact of environmental factors on urban development
      through spatial association with land use types, studies on volumetric
      urban environments across diverse floor uses remain limited. This study
      applied geospatial intelligence to analyse the location characteristics of
      space uses, extracting 90 environment features to interpret
      intra-interactions of the complex urban system, providing insights into
      the shape of urban form and informing smart governance. Using tree-based
      AI ensemble algorithms, Random Forest and eXtreme Gradient Boosting
      models, we examine the spatial effects of environmental factors on space
      parcels for being 15 different uses. Feature importance and partial
      dependence analysis of each space parcel for each factor assist in mapping
      the spatial suitability and provide insights into the determinants of
      space uses in mixed-use cities. Furthermore, we map the suitability of
      space parcels for residential, visitor accommodation, office and diet use,
      visualising the combined effect of environmental features. The findings
      contribute to informed decision-making for the optimal use of urban space
      in modern compact cities.
