TI  - Twin Minds in Code Education: Simulating Student-Tutor Dynamics using LLMs
      as Cognitive Digital Twins
SP  - 88-93
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.
AB  - The integration of Agentic AI in educational settings offers unprecedented
      opportunities for personalized learning. This study explores the concept
      of Twin Minds by employing Large Language Models as Cognitive Digital
      Twins of computer programming students. We propose a multi-agent framework
      where an Agentic Tutor interacts autonomously with a simulated Student
      Twin. To evaluate pedagogical strategies and behavioral resistance in a
      simulated environment, we conducted an exploratory dual ablation study.
      First, we compared a Socratic Tutor against a baseline Direct Tutor.
      Second, we evaluated the framework across two distinct student personas: a
      receptive Novice Student and a resistant Stubborn Student. An automated
      LLM Judge verified code correctness across 30 tasks divided into
      Foundational and Advanced Algorithmic tiers. The Socratic Tutor achieved
      an 85.00% success rate on foundational tasks and 100.00% on advanced tasks
      for the receptive novice. In contrast, providing direct code caused
      integration failures, dropping foundational success to 40.00%.
      Furthermore, our behavioral ablation reveals a preliminary observation we
      term "alignment override,"where instruction-tuned LLMs tend to abandon
      negative personas when presented with logical hints. This research
      contributes exploratory insights into multi-agent pedagogical modeling.
      The findings highlight the need for further research into developing
      scalable, resilient AI tutoring systems capable of maintaining educational
      integrity against uncooperative learner behaviors. © 2026 IEEE.
