TI  - An Ontology-Driven Framework for Adaptive and Transparent AI-Assisted
      Student Assessment
VL  - 583 LNBIP
SP  - 246-267
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed educational assessment
      through real-time feedback, adaptive learning, and automated evaluation
      mechanisms. However, existing AI-driven assessment models primarily focus
      on correctness-based grading, failing to capture cognitive engagement,
      problem-solving strategies, and AI-assisted modifications. This study
      introduces IntelliFrame, an ontology-based AI-driven framework designed to
      improve accuracy, adaptability, and transparency in AI-assisted
      assessments across multiple disciplines. Unlike traditional models,
      IntelliFrame systematically evaluates student interactions with AI tools,
      tracking decision-making processes and iterative learning behaviors to
      enable process-oriented assessment. A controlled study with 312
      undergraduate students demonstrated significant cognitive improvements,
      including a 32% increase in structured problem-solving (p < 0.01, Cohen’s
      d = 0.78), an 18% gain in independent critical reasoning (p < 0.05,
      Cohen’s d = 0.62), and a 27% enhancement in task refinement ability (p <
      0.01, Cohen’s d = 0.71). Additionally, 81% of students found
      IntelliFrame’s adaptive feedback mechanisms beneficial, while 74% improved
      their ability to assess AI-generated content critically. Faculty members
      observed a 42% reduced grading workload, highlighting IntelliFrame’s
      impact on assessment efficiency and real-time progress tracking. Designed
      for scalability and cross-disciplinary application, IntelliFrame extends
      beyond programming education to business analytics, legal studies, and
      medical education. It integrates seamlessly with Intelligent Tutoring
      Systems (ITS) and AI-driven peer assessment models, further enhancing
      personalized learning pathways. While IntelliFrame improves adaptive
      assessment accuracy, challenges remain in bias mitigation, explainability
      of AI-generated feedback, and optimizing task complexity. Future research
      should refine bias-detection algorithms, enhance interoperability with
      learning analytics platforms, and develop scaffolding techniques for
      AI-assisted learning. IntelliFrame provides a transparent, adaptable, and
      process-driven assessment framework, ensuring fairness, cognitive
      engagement, and discipline-specific adaptability in AI-enhanced education.
      © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG
      2026.
