TI  - A Virtual Simulation and AI-Enhanced CBL Model for Automotive Electrical
      Fault Diagnosis: System Design and Efficacy Study
SP  - 1014-1019
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery, Inc
AB  - This paper presents the design, implementation, and comprehensive
      evaluation of an intelligent virtual simulation platform deeply integrated
      with a Case-Based Learning (CBL) pedagogy, specifically developed for
      automotive electrical fault diagnosis training. The research addresses
      three critical challenges in traditional vocational training: high
      equipment costs, safety risks associated with physical fault insertion,
      and the limited diversity of fault scenarios available for practice. We
      developed a novel, data-driven virtual simulation system featuring three
      core modules: 1) a high-fidelity 3D interactive model library enabling
      immersive disassembly and assembly of vehicle components, 2) an
      AI-assisted intelligent fault case generation and management system
      (IFCGMS) that supports configurable compound faults through a rule-based
      reasoning and fault tree analysis model, and 3) a dynamic principle
      visualization and intelligent assessment module (DPVIAM) that tracks,
      evaluates, and provides real-time feedback on student operations. A
      quasi-experimental study was conducted, comparing an experimental group
      (N=33) using the integrated "virtual simulation-CBL"model against a
      control group (N=30) receiving traditional instruction. The results
      demonstrate a significant superiority of the experimental group, with a
      +12.2 point improvement in real-vehicle troubleshooting scores and a +9.6
      point gain in comprehensive course performance. Furthermore, in-depth
      analysis of platform log data using formal computational models -
      including a Diagnostic Sequence Similarity Score (DSSS) to evaluate
      diagnostic procedures and Pearson correlation analysis (r = 0.82) to link
      virtual and real performance - revealed that diagnostic efficiency and
      error correction patterns in the virtual environment are strong predictors
      of final practical competency. This work contributes a scalable,
      computer-aided instructional framework that effectively leverages virtual
      simulation, pedagogical design, and learning analytics, offering a
      replicable solution for enhancing skill acquisition in high-risk,
      high-cost technical training domains such as automotive diagnostics. ©
