TI  - Unequal Content: Search Engine Personalization Influences Exposure to
      Information Regarding the Israel-Palestine Conflict
T2  - IEEE Access
JF  - IEEE Access: Practical Innovations, Open Solutions
VL  - 14
SP  - 15347-15361
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
AB  - Search engines serve as primary gateways to information, and users tend to
      trust them, despite being opaque algorithms and customizing results in
      non-transparent ways. Personalization, such as tailoring content based on
      a user's location, is useful for routine content searches (e.g., finding
      nearby restaurants). However, this same personalization becomes
      problematic-and potentially harmful-when users seek information on
      sensitive or contentious geopolitical topics. In this work, we present the
      first large-scale measurement of how search engine curation and
      personalization affect users across different countries with distinct
      regional, political, and cultural traits. We developed anovel,
      privacy-preserving, bot-based methodology that simulates onlineuser
      profiles embedded with cumulative regional traits. These customized bots
      were deployed from different locations and conducted identical searches,
      either on the Israel-Palestine conflict (e.g. "Hamas") or neutral queries
      (e.g. "dinner recipe"), using both Google and DuckDuckGo. Our study
      addresses two main questions: 1) How do search results for the same query
      differ across countries, and which factors drive these differences?; and
      2) Does personalization vary more significantly for conflict-related or
      for general queries? Our findings show that search result rankings are
      significantly influenced by location, interface language, and simulated
      browsing context, with the strongest effects observed for conflict-related
      queries. In many cases, these differences led to non-neutral portrayals of
      the same event, depending on the user's presumed region. These results
      highlight how search engine personalization can shape public understanding
      of global events, creating fragmented or biased informational
      environments. This underscores the importance of educating users about the
      invisible filters that influence their access to information and, from a
      policy standpoint, these findings reveal a pressing need for greater
      transparency and accountability in how search engines personalize
      politically sensitive content. This is especially urgent given their
      outsized role in shaping democratic discourse and public opinion
      worldwide.
