TI  - Large Language Model-Based Multimodal Learning Analytics and Intelligent
      Path Planning
SP  - 415-419
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025
AB  - Online education generates vast multimodal learning data including text
      discussions, video interactions, audio recordings, and behavioral logs.
      Traditional learning analytics rely on single modalities and fixed
      learning paths, failing to capture comprehensive learning experiences and
      adapt to individual needs. This paper proposes a Large Language
      Model-based framework for multimodal learning analytics and intelligent
      path planning. Our approach leverages LLM semantic understanding to
      analyze heterogeneous data-forum discussions, video behaviors, speech
      interactions, clickstream patterns-constructing comprehensive learner
      cognitive states. The framework employs cross-modal attention fusion and
      LLM chain-of-thought reasoning to generate personalized learning paths
      that dynamically adapt to real-time performance. Experiments on Coursera
      dataset (15,000 learners, 6 months) demonstrate multimodal analytics
      achieves AUC 0.91 and accuracy 0.92, outperforming baselines by 7-9
      percentage points. Randomized controlled trial with 500 participants over
      8 weeks validates intelligent path planning, showing 17.3 % learning
      efficiency improvement, 6.3 % knowledge mastery increase, and 12
      percentage point higher completion rate versus fixed curricula.
      Weaker-foundation learners benefit most substantially (11.2 % knowledge
      gain), validating the framework's capacity to reduce educational
      inequality through adaptive personalization. © 2025 IEEE.
