AB  - This paper explores how adaptive, AI-based learning ecosystems help to
      improve student engagement and personalization of the course in hybrid
      (mixture of face-to-face and online) higher education. Based on the ideas
      of adaptive hypermedia, intelligent tutoring, and learning analytics
      research, we created an ecosystem that includes (a) a recommendation
      module to suggest content in a predictive approach, (b) an engagement
      monitor capable of estimating affective states, and (c) a dashboard to
      provide teachers with real-time pedagogical decisions. The
      quasi-experimental research used a mixed-methods approach and investigated
      differences between 3 universities, where N = 312 undergraduates would
      study in the control group (traditional blended instruction), and the
      experimental group (AI-driven ecosystem). Quantitative data consisted of
      learning gain (pre/post-tests), LMS activity measures and perceived
      personalization (Likert scales). Focus groups and instructor interviews
      formed qualitative data. Statistically significant improvements were found
      in learning gain (d = 0.45) and engagement metrics (time on task, number
      of reflective posts) across conditions in favour of the experimental
      condition; thematic analysis on qualitative data showed positivity toward
      adjustable pacing and feedback-on-demand. We address the implications on
      instructional design, equity, and ethics and introduce a framework that
      can be used to implement the AI ecosystems in a responsible way in a
      hybrid space. © 2025 IEEE.
