TI  - Machine Learning Based Insights into the Pedagogical Shifts in the STEM
      Education: From Rote Learning to Critical Thinking
VL  - 1598 LNEE
SP  - 532-549
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026
PB  - Springer Science and Business Media Deutschland GmbH
AB  - The accelerating speed of the growth of the activity of the Machine
      Learning (ML) in the field of education is altering the process of
      teaching and learning of courses related to Science, Technology,
      Engineering, and Mathematics (STEM). However, despite being the most
      prevalent historically, conventional ways of rote memorization, are slowly
      being interpreted as insufficient in providing students with problem
      solving and innovation capabilities. ML-driven analytics are useful to
      comprehend both teaching and learning in ways that can be used to design
      pedagogy capable of developing the four higher-order skills of critical
      thinking, creativity, and collaboration. According to the United Nations
      Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), these changes directly support SDG
      4: Quality Education, namely through encouraging inclusive and equitable
      and skills-based teaching, and SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and
      Infrastructure, which involves developing an education system oriented to
      work in the innovation-driven economies. This article explores how ML can
      be used to find out the best instructional practices or help create a
      learning environment that emphasize adaptive learning to help learners go
      beyond memorization. It discusses the existing uses of ML in STEM
      education, such as learning analytics, predictive modeling, and
      intelligent tutoring systems, and considers examples of case studies in
      which the use of data-driven insights has transformed classroom practices.
      The analysis mentions two opportunities, which are increased
      personalization, the possibility to identify learning gaps early, and the
      ability to design the curriculum based on evidence, as well as challenges,
      such as biases in algorithms, disparities in access to technology, and the
      necessity of the teacher being ready. The paper fronts this thesis by
      explaining the contextualisation of ML-based educational innovation
      between pedagogical reform and sustainable development, and as such argues
      that responsible harnessing of ML can expedite the shift of rote to
      critical learning, providing the cognitive and creative capacity of
      learners that can be beneficial to the knowledge economies of the 21st
      century. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature
      Switzerland AG 2026.
